window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-chem-periodicity","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity · Practice","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The first four successive ionization enthalpies of a main-group element M are 578, 1817, 2745 and 11577 kJ $mol^{-1}$. What is the most likely formula of its normal oxide?","image":"","options":["$MO$","$M_2O_3$","$MO_2$","$M_2O$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Successive ionization enthalpies rise gradually while valence electrons are being removed.\nThe jump from 2745 to 11577 kJ $mol^{-1}$ is very large, so the fourth electron must come from a noble-gas core.\nM therefore has 3 valence electrons (group 13) and shows valence 3.\nWith oxygen (valence 2), the oxide is $M_2O_3$, like $Al_2O_3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Element 119 has not yet been discovered. Based on the trends in this chapter, how many of the following predictions about it are correct?\n(i) Its temporary IUPAC name is ununennium, with symbol Uue.\n(ii) It would be an s-block element of Group 1.\n(iii) It would have a higher first ionization enthalpy than caesium.\n(iv) Its normal oxide would have the formula $M_2O$.\n(v) It would be the first element of the seventh period.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the roots are un-un-enn, so the name is ununennium and the symbol is Uue.\n(ii) Correct: after Og (Z = 118), the next electron enters 8s, giving $[Og]8s^1$. This places it in Group 1.\n(iii) Incorrect: ionization enthalpy decreases down a group, so it should be lower than that of Cs.\n(iv) Correct: with valence 1, its oxide would be $M_2O$, like $Na_2O$.\n(v) Incorrect: the seventh period ends at Z = 118, so it would start the eighth period.\nThree predictions are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mendeleev left some gaps in his periodic table, for example one under aluminium and one under silicon.\nReason (R): Mendeleev arranged elements strictly in increasing order of atomic weight and never departed from this order.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Mendeleev left gaps for undiscovered elements, which he called eka-aluminium and eka-silicon. These were later found to be Ga and Ge.\nR is false. He sometimes ignored the atomic weight order to keep similar elements together. For example, he placed iodine after tellurium.\nThe gaps actually arose because he gave priority to similarity of properties over a strict weight sequence.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"An element E has atomic number 34. Based on its position in the periodic table and the valence trends of its group, which pair correctly gives the formula of its hydride and of its oxide in which it shows its highest valence?","image":"","options":["$EH_4$ and $EO_2$","$EH_3$ and $E_2O_5$","$H_2E$ and $EO_3$","$HE$ and $E_2O_7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The configuration for Z = 34 is $[Ar]3d^{10}4s^24p^4$. This puts E in period 4, group 16 (it is selenium).\nA group 16 element has 6 valence electrons, so its valences are $8 - 6 = 2$ and 6.\nWith hydrogen it uses valence 2, giving $H_2E$ (like $H_2Se$).\nIts highest valence is 6, giving $EO_3$ (like $SeO_3$).\n$EH_3$ / $E_2O_5$ fits group 15, and $HE$ / $E_2O_7$ fits group 17.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (Pauling electronegativity).\nList-I: (A) N (B) Si (C) Al (D) Br\nList-II: (i) 1.5 (ii) 3.0 (iii) 1.8 (iv) 2.8","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"N (period 2, group 15) has 3.0, so A matches (ii).\nSi (period 3, group 14) has 1.8, so B matches (iii).\nAl lies to the left of Si, so its value is lower at 1.5. C matches (i).\nBr lies below Cl (3.0), so its value is slightly lower at 2.8. D matches (iv).\nOption (3) swaps Si and Al, which would make Al more electronegative than Si. That goes against the period trend.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following elements is classified as a metalloid (semi-metal)?","image":"","options":["Phosphorus","Arsenic","Bismuth","Gallium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Metalloids lie along the zig-zag line between metals and non-metals and show properties of both.\nNCERT lists silicon, germanium, arsenic, antimony and tellurium as metalloids, so arsenic fits.\nPhosphorus is a non-metal.\nBismuth and gallium are metals. Gallium is tempting because it melts at about 303 K, but a low melting point does not make it a metalloid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The second ionization enthalpy of sodium is higher than that of magnesium.\n(ii) The electronegativity of nitrogen is 3.0 on the Pauling scale in all of its compounds.\n(iii) The maximum covalency of boron is 4, because only the 2s and 2p orbitals are available for bonding.\n(iv) In $[AlCl(H_2O)_5]^{2+}$, the oxidation state and the covalency of Al are both 3.\n(v) Two isotopes of the same element have practically the same first ionization enthalpy.\n(vi) Helium is strictly an s-block element, but it is placed in group 18 because its valence shell is completely filled.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Na$^+$ has a stable noble gas core, while Mg$^+$ still has one 3s electron to lose.\n(ii) Incorrect: electronegativity is not constant. It varies with the atom an element is bonded to.\n(iii) Correct: a second-period element has only 4 valence orbitals, so boron forms $BF_4^-$ but not $BF_6^{3-}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the oxidation state of Al is +3, but its covalency is 6.\n(v) Correct: isotopes differ only in nuclear mass. Nuclear charge and electron configuration are identical.\n(vi) Correct: helium has a $1s^2$ closed shell.\nCorrect statements: (i), (iii), (v), (vi), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The fifth period of the Periodic Table contains 18 elements.\nReason (R): In the fifth period, electrons fill the 5s, 4d and 5p orbitals, which together number 9 orbitals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Period 5 runs from Rb (Z = 37) to Xe (Z = 54), which is 18 elements.\nR is true. The orbitals are filled in the energy order 5s < 4d < 5p, giving 1 + 5 + 3 = 9 orbitals.\nNine orbitals can hold 18 electrons, so R correctly explains why period 5 has 18 elements.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Metals make up more than 78% of all known elements.\n(b) Non-metals are fewer than twenty in number and lie at the top right of the periodic table.\n(c) The change from metallic to non-metallic character along a period is abrupt, occurring sharply at the zig-zag line.\n(d) Germanium and tellurium are metalloids.\n(e) All non-metals have low melting and boiling points, without exception.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: metals make up more than 78% of the known elements.\n(b) Correct: NCERT says non-metals are less than twenty in number and lie at the top right.\n(c) Incorrect: the change is gradual. The metalloids along the zig-zag line show intermediate properties.\n(d) Correct: Ge and Te are listed among the metalloids.\n(e) Incorrect: boron and carbon are non-metals with high melting points.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match the outer electronic configuration in List-I with the group in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $ns^2np^5$\n(b) $ns^2np^4$\n(c) $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$\n(d) $ns^2$ (helium excluded)\nList-II\n(i) Chalcogens (Group 16)\n(ii) Group 12\n(iii) Halogens (Group 17)\n(iv) Alkaline earth metals (Group 2)","image":"","options":["a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $ns^2np^5$ has 7 valence electrons, so it is group 17, the halogens: (iii).\n(b) $ns^2np^4$ has 6 valence electrons, so it is group 16, the chalcogens: (i).\n(c) $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$ is the last d-block column, group 12 (Zn, Cd, Hg): (ii).\n(d) $ns^2$ with helium excluded is the group 2 alkaline earth metals: (iv).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The first ionization enthalpy of oxygen is lower than that of nitrogen.\nReason (R): During the first ionization of oxygen, the electron is removed from a 2s orbital, which is less tightly held than a 2p orbital.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: O has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than N.\nR is false on two counts. First, the electron removed from O comes from 2p, not 2s. Second, a 2s electron penetrates closer to the nucleus and is held more tightly than a 2p electron.\nThe actual reason: in O ($2p^4$), two electrons share one 2p orbital, so electron–electron repulsion increases. N ($2p^3$) has three singly occupied orbitals (Hund's rule).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes electronegativity from ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy?","image":"","options":["Electronegativity is measured experimentally in kJ $mol^{-1}$ for isolated gaseous atoms","Electronegativity is a qualitative measure for an atom in a compound and is not directly measurable","Electronegativity refers to an isolated atom, while the other two refer to bonded atoms","Electronegativity has a fixed value for an element in all its compounds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ionization enthalpy and electron gain enthalpy are measurable energy changes of isolated gaseous atoms.\nElectronegativity is a qualitative measure of how strongly an atom in a compound attracts shared electrons. It is not a measurable quantity.\nOption (1) describes the enthalpies, not electronegativity.\nOption (3) reverses the situation, and option (4) is false because electronegativity changes with the bonded partner.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following oxides are neutral, showing neither acidic nor basic properties?\nCO, Na$_2$O, NO, Al$_2$O$_3$, N$_2$O, Cl$_2$O$_7$, As$_2$O$_3$, MgO","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Neutral oxides: CO, NO and N$_2$O, which makes 3.\nAmphoteric oxides: Al$_2$O$_3$ and As$_2$O$_3$. They act as acids towards bases and as bases towards acids, so they are not neutral.\nBasic oxides: Na$_2$O and MgO (elements on the left of the table).\nAcidic oxide: Cl$_2$O$_7$, which gives HClO$_4$ with water.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"On the Pauling scale, which value was arbitrarily assigned to fluorine, the element considered to have the greatest ability to attract electrons?","image":"","options":["3.0","3.5","4.0","4.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In 1922 Linus Pauling arbitrarily gave fluorine a value of 4.0.\nOther elements were placed on the scale relative to this value. For example, oxygen is 3.5 and chlorine and nitrogen are 3.0.\nThe value 3.5 belongs to oxygen, so it is a tempting but wrong choice.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The number of elements in a period is twice the number of atomic orbitals being filled in that period. How many atomic orbitals are filled across the sixth period?","image":"","options":["9","16","18","32"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In period 6, electrons successively enter the 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p orbitals.\nCounting orbitals: 6s = 1, 4f = 7, 5d = 5 and 6p = 3, which gives 16 orbitals.\nEach orbital holds 2 electrons, so period 6 has 2 × 16 = 32 elements.\nThe value 32 counts elements, not orbitals. The value 9 applies to periods 4 and 5.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Lead (Z = 82) is a group 14 element of the sixth period. The element lying directly below lead in the same group has which IUPAC systematic name and which official name?","image":"","options":["Unnilquadium; Rutherfordium","Ununpentium; Moscovium","Ununquadium; Flerovium","Ununoctium; Oganesson"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: The sixth period contains 32 elements. So the element below Pb in the seventh period has Z = 82 + 32 = 114.\nStep 2: The digits 1, 1, 4 give un-un-quad, so the systematic name is Ununquadium (Uuq).\nStep 3: Its official name is Flerovium (Fl).\nStep 4: Unnilquadium (104) is in group 4, not 14. Moscovium is 115 and Oganesson is 118.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"A newly reported element has atomic number 119. Using IUPAC systematic nomenclature, what are its temporary name and symbol?","image":"","options":["Ununennium, Uue","Unennium, Une","Ununnonium, Uun","Unbinilium, Ubn"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Write the digits of the atomic number in order: 1, 1, 9.\nStep 2: The IUPAC roots are 1 = un, 1 = un and 9 = enn. Joining them and adding 'ium' gives Ununennium.\nStep 3: The symbol uses the initial letter of each root: U, u, e, giving Uue.\nStep 4: 'Ubn' (unbinilium) is the name for element 120. 'Unennium' leaves out one root. 'Nonium' is not an IUPAC root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the early attempts at classifying elements are correct?\n(i) Between 1800 and 1865 the number of known elements more than doubled, from 31 to 63.\n(ii) de Chancourtois, a French geologist, displayed the periodic recurrence of properties using a cylindrical table of elements arranged by increasing atomic weight.\n(iii) Newlands' Law of Octaves held well for elements heavier than calcium.\n(iv) Lothar Meyer, unlike Newlands, observed that the length of the repeating pattern changes along the series of elements.\n(v) Dobereiner's Law of Triads was accepted as a general law because it applied to all the elements known at that time.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 31 elements were known in 1800 and 63 by 1865.\n(ii) Correct: In 1862 de Chancourtois arranged elements by atomic weight on a cylindrical table.\n(iii) Incorrect: The Law of Octaves seemed to work only for elements up to calcium.\n(iv) Correct: Lothar Meyer's curves showed that the length of the repeating pattern changes.\n(v) Incorrect: The triad relation worked for only a few elements, so it was dismissed as coincidence.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The internuclear distance in a gaseous $Br_2$ molecule, where the two bromine atoms are joined by a single covalent bond, is 228 pm. What is the covalent radius of bromine?","image":"","options":["114 pm","228 pm","57 pm","456 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Br–Br single-bond distance = 228 pm.\nFormula: covalent radius = $\\frac{1}{2}$ × internuclear distance between two identical atoms joined by a single bond.\nSubstituting: r = 228/2 = 114 pm.\nThis matches the NCERT value for Br in Table 3.6(b).\nSo the covalent radius of Br = 114 pm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Element X lies in the same group as indium (Z = 49) and in the period immediately below it. What is the atomic number of X?","image":"","options":["67","81","85","113"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Indium has Z = 49 and its valence shell is n = 5, so it is in period 5, group 13.\nThe element directly below it is in period 6, group 13.\nTo move down one group position, add the number of elements in the next period. Period 6 contains 32 elements because it includes the 14 lanthanoids.\nSo Z = 49 + 32 = 81 (thallium).\nAdding only 18 would give 67, which is a lanthanoid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The second electron gain enthalpy of oxygen, for the process $O^-(g) + e^- \\rightarrow O^{2-}(g)$, is positive.\nReason (R): The incoming electron is added to a negatively charged ion and is repelled by it, so energy must be supplied to add it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Assertion: the first electron gain enthalpy of O is negative (–141 kJ mol$^{-1}$). Adding a second electron, however, requires energy, so the second value is positive. A is true.\nReason: the second electron approaches the $O^-$ ion, which already carries a negative charge. The resulting electrostatic repulsion must be overcome by supplying energy. R is true.\nThis repulsion is exactly why the second step is endothermic, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (outer electronic configuration) with List-II (family):\nList-I:\n(A) $ns^2np^5$\n(B) $ns^2np^4$\n(C) $ns^1$\n(D) $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$\nList-II:\n(i) Alkali metals\n(ii) Chalcogens\n(iii) Halogens\n(iv) Group 12 metals lacking most transition properties","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A: $ns^2np^5$ has seven valence electrons, which is group 17 (halogens). So A-iii.\nB: $ns^2np^4$ has six valence electrons, which is group 16 (chalcogens). So B-ii.\nC: $ns^1$ is the group 1 alkali-metal configuration. So C-i.\nD: $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$ belongs to Zn, Cd and Hg, which lack most transition-metal properties. So D-iv.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"According to the IUPAC systematic nomenclature for elements not yet officially named, what would be the temporary name and symbol of the element with atomic number 126?","image":"","options":["Unbihexium, Ubh","Unbihexium, Ubx","Ununhexium, Uuh","Bibihexium, Bbh"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The roots are un = 1, bi = 2 and hex = 6, written in the order of the digits 1-2-6, with 'ium' added at the end. This gives unbihexium.\nThe symbol uses the first letter of each root: U, b, h, giving Ubh.\nUnunhexium (Uuh) was the temporary name for element 116, not 126.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the sixth period, filling of the 4f orbitals begins with cerium (Z = 58) after the 6s orbital is filled.\nStatement II: The fifth period begins with rubidium, and its 4d transition series starts at zirconium (Z = 40).","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. In period 6 the filling order is 6s, then 4f, 5d and 6p, and 4f filling begins at Ce (Z = 58).\nStatement II is false. Period 5 does begin at Rb, but the 4d series starts at yttrium (Z = 39, $4d^{1}5s^{2}$), not zirconium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (IUPAC temporary name) with List-II (atomic number).\nList-I:\n(A) Unnilquadium\n(B) Unnilennium\n(C) Ununbium\n(D) Ununoctium\nList-II:\n(i) 118\n(ii) 112\n(iii) 109\n(iv) 104","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The numerical roots are: nil = 0, un = 1, bi = 2, quad = 4, oct = 8, enn = 9.\n(A) un-nil-quad gives 104.\n(B) un-nil-enn gives 109.\n(C) un-un-bi gives 112.\n(D) un-un-oct gives 118.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In his periodic table, Mendeleev placed iodine in the same group as fluorine, chlorine and bromine, even though iodine has a lower atomic weight than tellurium.\nReason (R): Mendeleev gave more importance to the similarity of properties of elements than to strictly following the order of atomic weights.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: iodine (lower atomic weight) was placed after tellurium, in Group VII with F, Cl and Br.\nR is true: Mendeleev believed some atomic weights might be wrong. He kept elements with similar properties in the same group.\nR is exactly why he broke the atomic weight order for iodine, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Palladium has the outer configuration $4d^{10}5s^0$, an exception to the usual d-block pattern.\n(ii) Metals make up more than 78% of all known elements.\n(iii) The s-block and p-block elements together are called representative or main group elements.\n(iv) Halogens and chalcogens have highly negative electron gain enthalpies and readily add one and two electrons, respectively.\n(v) Every d-block element forms coloured ions and is paramagnetic.\n(vi) The lanthanoid and actinoid series contain both metals and non-metals.","image":"","options":["3","5","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Pd is $4d^{10}5s^0$, with no s-electron.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT states that metals are more than 78% of known elements.\n(iii) Correct: p-block (groups 13-18) plus s-block form the representative elements.\n(iv) Correct: halogens gain 1 electron and chalcogens gain 2 electrons to attain a noble gas configuration.\n(v) Incorrect: Zn, Cd and Hg ($d^{10}$) do not show most transition properties.\n(vi) Incorrect: all f-block elements are metals.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The atomic radii of noble gases are not listed alongside the covalent radii of other elements in a period. With which quantity should the radii of noble gases properly be compared?","image":"","options":["Metallic radii of the alkali metals","Van der Waals radii of other elements","Ionic radii of the halide ions","Covalent radii of the halogens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Noble gases are monoatomic, so they form no covalent or metallic bonds. Their radii are non-bonded radii and are very large.\nA non-bonded radius must be compared with another non-bonded radius, that is, the van der Waals radius of other elements.\nComparing them with covalent radii, even those of the neighbouring halogens, is not a like-for-like comparison.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the f-block elements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The lanthanoids extend from Ce (Z = 58) to Lu (Z = 71).","All actinoid elements are radioactive.","The chemistry of the early actinoids is simpler than that of the corresponding lanthanoids, because actinoids show fewer oxidation states.","Elements that come after uranium are called transuranium elements."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the 4f series runs from Ce (58) to Lu (71).\n(2) Correct: NCERT states that actinoid elements are radioactive.\n(3) Incorrect: the early actinoids have more complicated chemistry than the corresponding lanthanoids. This is because they can show a large number of oxidation states, not fewer.\n(4) Correct: elements beyond uranium (Z = 92) are called transuranium elements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Following the IUPAC digit-root rules, the three-letter temporary symbol for the element with atomic number 119 would be:","image":"","options":["Uun","Une","Uue","Uen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: The digits of 119 are 1, 1 and 9, with roots un, un and enn.\nStep 2: The symbol is made from the first letters of these roots, in order: U, u, e. This gives Uue.\nStep 3: Uun is element 110 (un-un-nil) and Une is element 109 (un-nil-enn). Uen does not follow the digit order.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Newlands' Law of Octaves was found to hold only for elements up to:","image":"","options":["Fluorine","Chlorine","Potassium","Calcium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Newlands arranged the elements by increasing atomic weight. He noted that every eighth element resembled the first, much like musical octaves.\nNCERT states that this relationship seemed to be true only for elements up to calcium. Beyond calcium, the regular repetition after every eighth element breaks down.\nPotassium is a tempting choice, but Ca follows K in his table, so the law still worked at K.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The atomic radius decreases from lithium (152 pm) to fluorine (64 pm) across the second period.\nStatement II: Across a period, the valence electrons enter the same shell while the effective nuclear charge increases.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Table 3.6(a) shows the radius falling steadily from Li (152 pm) to F (64 pm).\nStatement II is correct. In a period, the electrons are added to the same valence shell, and the effective nuclear charge rises with Z. The nucleus therefore pulls the electrons closer.\nStatement II is in fact the reason for Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about ionization enthalpy is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Beryllium has a higher first ionization enthalpy than boron because a 2s electron penetrates closer to the nucleus than a 2p electron.","Oxygen has a lower first ionization enthalpy than nitrogen because of extra repulsion between the paired 2p electrons in oxygen.","Down a group, ionization enthalpy decreases because increased shielding outweighs the increase in nuclear charge.","The second ionization enthalpy of an element is lower than the first because the positive ion is smaller than the atom."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. The 2p electron of boron is more shielded than the 2s electron of beryllium, so it is easier to remove.\n(2) Correct. Two of oxygen's four 2p electrons share one orbital, and the extra repulsion makes one of them easier to remove.\n(3) Correct. This is the NCERT explanation for the trend down a group.\n(4) Incorrect. Removing an electron from a positive ion is harder, so $\\Delta_i H_2 > \\Delta_i H_1$ always.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Hydrogen is placed separately at the top of the long form of the Periodic Table because it can behave like the alkali metals as well as like the halogens.\nStatement II: Helium strictly belongs to the s-block, but it is placed in group 18 because its valence shell $(1s^2)$ is completely filled.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Hydrogen has one s-electron, like the group 1 metals. It can also gain one electron to reach a noble gas configuration, like the group 17 elements. Being a special case, it is placed separately.\nStatement II is correct. Helium's last electron enters the 1s orbital, so it is an s-block element. Its closed $1s^2$ shell gives it noble gas properties, which justifies placing it in group 18.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Electronegativity increases from lithium to fluorine across the second period.\nReason (R): Across a period the atomic radius decreases, so the attraction between the valence electrons and the nucleus increases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The Pauling values rise from Li 1.0 to F 4.0.\nR is true. Effective nuclear charge increases across the period, so atoms become smaller.\nIn a smaller atom the nucleus attracts shared electrons more strongly, which raises the electronegativity.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following processes has an enthalpy change equal to the first ionization enthalpy of magnesium?","image":"","options":["$Mg(g) \\rightarrow Mg^{+}(g) + e^{-}$","$Mg(s) \\rightarrow Mg^{+}(g) + e^{-}$","$Mg(g) \\rightarrow Mg^{2+}(g) + 2e^{-}$","$Mg^{+}(g) + e^{-} \\rightarrow Mg(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First ionization enthalpy is the energy needed to remove one electron from an isolated gaseous atom in its ground state.\nOption (1) removes one electron from a gaseous atom, so it fits the definition.\nOption (2) starts from solid Mg, so it also includes the enthalpy of sublimation.\nOption (3) removes two electrons, which is the sum of the first and second ionization enthalpies.\nOption (4) is the reverse process, in which energy is released.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The number of elements in a period is twice the number of atomic orbitals available in the energy level being filled.\n(ii) The 4f-inner transition series belongs to the fifth period.\n(iii) The element with Z = 120 would have the configuration $[Og]\\,8s^{2}$ and belong to group 2.\n(iv) The 5f-inner transition (actinoid) series results from filling of 5f orbitals after actinium (Z = 89).\n(v) The fourth period has 18 elements because the 4s, 4p and 4d orbitals are filled in it.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. For example, period 4 fills 9 orbitals and has 18 elements.\n(ii) Incorrect. The 4f filling (Ce to Lu) occurs in the sixth period.\n(iii) Correct. Element 120 adds two electrons to 8s after oganesson, making it an alkaline earth metal.\n(iv) Correct. NCERT states that the 5f series follows actinium.\n(v) Incorrect. Period 4 fills 4s, 3d and 4p; 4d is filled in period 5.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The first ionization enthalpy of sodium is 496 kJ $mol^{-1}$. What is the energy required to ionize a single gaseous sodium atom? ($N_A = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}$ $mol^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$8.24 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$8.24 \\times 10^{-20}$ J","$4.96 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$8.24 \\times 10^{-18}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_i H$ = 496 kJ $mol^{-1}$ = $4.96 \\times 10^{5}$ J $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: energy per atom = $\\frac{\\Delta_i H}{N_A}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{4.96 \\times 10^{5}}{6.022 \\times 10^{23}}$.\nSo the energy per atom = $8.24 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How do the first ionization enthalpies of the isotopes $^{35}Cl$ and $^{37}Cl$ compare?","image":"","options":["They are the same, because both isotopes have the same nuclear charge and the same electronic configuration.","$^{37}Cl$ has the higher value, because its heavier nucleus holds the electrons more strongly.","$^{35}Cl$ has the higher value, because its smaller nucleus lets the electrons come closer.","They differ, because the extra neutrons in $^{37}Cl$ change the number of electrons."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ionization enthalpy depends on the nuclear charge (number of protons) and on how the electrons are arranged.\nIsotopes have the same number of protons and electrons, and the same electronic configuration.\nNeutrons add mass but no charge, so nuclear mass does not affect the valence electrons.\nOption (2) is tempting, but a heavier nucleus does not exert a greater electrostatic pull.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mendeleev predicted a low melting point for eka-aluminium, and gallium was found to melt at about 303 K.\nStatement II: The density Mendeleev predicted for eka-silicon was slightly higher than the density actually found for germanium.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. The predicted melting point for eka-aluminium was 'low', and gallium's melting point is 302.93 K (about 303 K).\nStatement II: Correct. The predicted density was 5.5 g/cm$^3$ and germanium's found density is 5.36 g/cm$^3$. So the prediction was slightly higher.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Iodine has a lower atomic weight than tellurium, yet Mendeleev placed iodine in Group VII along with fluorine, chlorine and bromine. What was the basis of this placement?","image":"","options":["He believed iodine actually had a higher atomic weight than tellurium, so the weight order was preserved","Iodine resembles fluorine, chlorine and bromine in its properties","He knew that the atomic number of iodine is greater than that of tellurium","He used Moseley's X-ray frequency data for iodine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mendeleev's primary aim was to keep elements with similar properties in the same group.\nIodine clearly resembles F, Cl and Br. So he set aside the atomic-weight order and placed iodine after tellurium. He suspected that some atomic weight measurements might be incorrect.\nAtomic numbers and Moseley's work (1913) were unknown to him, so options 3 and 4 fail.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution):\nList-I\n(a) Dobereiner\n(b) Newlands\n(c) Lothar Meyer\n(d) Moseley\nList-II\n(i) Plotted atomic volume against atomic weight and found a repeating pattern of changing length\n(ii) Groups of three elements, where the middle atomic weight is roughly the average of the other two\n(iii) Linear plot of $\\sqrt{\\nu}$ of X-rays against atomic number\n(iv) Every eighth element resembles the first; this works only up to calcium","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Dobereiner proposed the Law of Triads, matching (ii).\n(b) Newlands proposed the Law of Octaves, which held only up to Ca, matching (iv).\n(c) Lothar Meyer plotted physical properties such as atomic volume against atomic weight and noticed that the length of the repeating pattern changed, matching (i).\n(d) Moseley found that $\\sqrt{\\nu}$ plotted against Z gives a straight line, showing atomic number is more fundamental, matching (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The formula Mendeleev predicted for the chloride of eka-aluminium (E) was:","image":"","options":["ECl$_2$","ECl$_3$","ECl$_4$","ECl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Eka-aluminium sits below aluminium, so Mendeleev expected it to form compounds like those of aluminium.\nHe predicted the oxide E$_2$O$_3$ and the chloride ECl$_3$. Gallium was later found to form Ga$_2$O$_3$ and GaCl$_3$.\nECl$_4$ (with oxide EO$_2$) was his prediction for eka-silicon, not eka-aluminium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly shows the increasing order of first ionization enthalpy for the third period elements Na, Mg, Al, Si, P and S?","image":"","options":["Na < Mg < Al < Si < P < S","Na < Al < Mg < Si < S < P","Na < Al < Mg < Si < P < S","Na < Mg < Al < Si < S < P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The general trend is that ionization enthalpy increases across a period. Na is the lowest at about 496 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAl is lower than Mg. The 3p electron of Al is more shielded and penetrates less than the 3s electron of Mg (about 577 vs 737 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nS is lower than P. P has a stable half-filled $3p^3$ configuration, while S has one paired 3p electron with extra repulsion. This parallels the O < N case.\nSi (786 kJ mol$^{-1}$) lies between Mg and S.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The element with $Z = 114$ lies in period 7 and group 14.\n(ii) The element with $Z = 117$ has the configuration $[Rn]5f^{14}6d^{10}7s^2 7p^5$ and belongs to group 17.\n(iii) The element with $Z = 120$ would have the valence configuration $8s^1$ and be an alkali metal.\n(iv) The seventh period ends at the element with $Z = 118$, which belongs to the noble gas family.\n(v) The element with $Z = 114$ is a d-block element because its 6d subshell is completely filled.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. $Z = 114$ is $[Rn]5f^{14}6d^{10}7s^2 7p^2$. Here n = 7 and $ns^2np^2$ places it in group 14.\n(ii) Correct. The $7p^5$ configuration gives a halogen-type element in group 17.\n(iii) Incorrect. 120 = 118 + 2, so the configuration is $[Og]8s^2$. This places it in group 2, the alkaline earth metals.\n(iv) Correct. Element 118 completes the 7p subshell and belongs to the noble gas family.\n(v) Incorrect. The last electron of element 114 enters 7p, so it is a p-block element.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Zn, Cd and Hg do not show most of the characteristic properties of transition elements, such as coloured ions and variable valence.\nReason (R): Zn, Cd and Hg have the outer electronic configuration $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that Zn, Cd and Hg do not show most transition-metal properties, such as coloured ions, variable oxidation states, paramagnetism and catalytic use.\nR is true. Their configuration is $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$.\nThe completely filled d-subshell is the reason they behave unlike typical transition metals. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Mendeleev predicted an atomic weight of 68 for eka-aluminium. The atomic weight found for gallium was 70. What is the percentage error of the prediction, taken relative to the experimentally found value?","image":"","options":["2.86 %","2.94 %","3.00 %","2.00 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: predicted value = 68, found value = 70.\nFormula: % error = $\\frac{|found - predicted|}{found} \\times 100$\nSubstituting: % error = $\\frac{2}{70} \\times 100$\nSo % error = 2.86 %.\nDividing by the predicted value (68) instead gives 2.94 %, which is the trap option.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"According to the Pauling electronegativity values of group 1 elements given in NCERT, which two alkali metals have the same electronegativity?","image":"","options":["Li and Na","Na and K","K and Rb","Rb and Cs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The group 1 values are Li 1.0, Na 0.9, K 0.8, Rb 0.8 and Cs 0.7.\nOnly K and Rb share a value (0.8).\nThe overall trend is still a decrease down the group, because atomic radius increases.\nThe other pairs differ by 0.1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Mendeleev left gaps in his table and predicted the properties of eka-aluminium and eka-silicon. Match each predicted property in List-I with its predicted value in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Formula of the oxide of eka-aluminium\n(B) Formula of the chloride of eka-silicon\n(C) Density of eka-aluminium\n(D) Atomic weight of eka-silicon\nList-II\n(i) $ECl_4$\n(ii) $E_2O_3$\n(iii) 72\n(iv) 5.9 g/cm$^3$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Eka-aluminium (gallium) sits below Al, so its oxide is $E_2O_3$, like $Al_2O_3$: A-(ii).\n(B) Eka-silicon (germanium) sits below Si and is tetravalent, so its chloride is $ECl_4$: B-(i).\n(C) The predicted density of eka-aluminium was 5.9 g/cm$^3$; gallium was later found to have 5.94 g/cm$^3$: C-(iv).\n(D) The predicted atomic weight of eka-silicon was 72; germanium's measured value is 72.6: D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The electron affinity ($A_e$) of chlorine, defined at absolute zero, is 349 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Using the relation $\\Delta_{eg}H = -A_e - \\frac{5}{2}RT$, calculate the electron gain enthalpy of chlorine at 298 K. Take R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["–355.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$","–342.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$","–349.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+355.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_e$ = 349 kJ mol$^{-1}$, T = 298 K, R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta_{eg}H = -A_e - \\frac{5}{2}RT$\nCorrection term: $\\frac{5}{2}RT = 2.5 \\times 8.314 \\times 298$ J mol$^{-1}$ = 6194 J mol$^{-1}$ = 6.19 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_{eg}H = -349 - 6.19$\nSo $\\Delta_{eg}H$ = –355.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThe sign is negative because energy is released; electron affinity is positive by its opposite sign convention.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In a Dobereiner triad, the middle element has an atomic weight roughly halfway between the other two. Using atomic weights Ca = 40 and Ba = 137, what atomic weight does the Law of Triads predict for strontium?","image":"","options":["48.5","88.5","97.0","177.0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Ca = 40, Ba = 137, and Ca–Sr–Ba is a Dobereiner triad.\nFormula: predicted weight of the middle element = (first + last)/2.\nSubstituting: (40 + 137)/2 = 177/2.\nSo the predicted atomic weight of Sr = 88.5, close to the actual value of 88.\n177 is the sum, not the average, and 48.5 is half the difference.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the Modern Periodic Law and the long form of the Periodic Table:\n(i) Moseley's work established that atomic number is a more fundamental property of an element than its atomic mass.\n(ii) The period number corresponds to the highest principal quantum number (n) of the elements in that period.\n(iii) The fifth period contains 32 elements because the 4f orbitals are filled in it.\n(iv) On the basis of quantum numbers, the seventh period can have a theoretical maximum of 32 elements.\n(v) The lanthanoids, shown in a separate panel at the bottom of the table, belong to the seventh period.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the straight-line $\\sqrt{\\nu}$ vs Z plot established this.\n(ii) Correct: as stated in NCERT.\n(iii) Incorrect: the fifth period fills 5s, 4d and 5p (9 orbitals), giving 18 elements. The 4f orbitals fill in the sixth period.\n(iv) Correct: like the sixth period, the seventh period has a theoretical maximum of 32 elements.\n(v) Incorrect: lanthanoids (Ce–Lu) belong to the sixth period. The actinoids belong to the seventh.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The long form of the Periodic Table uses the 1984 IUPAC numbering of groups from 1 to 18. This replaced the older notation. Match the old group labels in List-I with the IUPAC group numbers in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) III B\n(b) I B\n(c) VII A\n(d) 0\nList-II\n(i) 17\n(ii) 18\n(iii) 3\n(iv) 11","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The old sequence across the table is IA, IIA, IIIB–VIIB, VIII (three columns), IB, IIB, IIIA–VIIA, 0.\n(a) III B is the first d-block column, so it is group 3, matching (iii).\n(b) I B comes right after the three VIII columns (groups 8, 9, 10), so it is group 11, matching (iv).\n(c) VII A is the halogen column, group 17, matching (i).\n(d) 0 is the noble gas column, group 18, matching (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In 1913, Henry Moseley studied the characteristic X-ray spectra of elements. Which plot gave a straight line and showed that atomic number is more fundamental than atomic mass? ($\\nu$ = frequency of the emitted X-rays)","image":"","options":["$\\nu$ against atomic number (Z)","$\\sqrt{\\nu}$ against atomic number (Z)","$\\sqrt{\\nu}$ against atomic mass","$\\nu^2$ against atomic number (Z)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Moseley plotted the square root of the X-ray frequency, $\\sqrt{\\nu}$, against atomic number Z and got a straight line.\nThe plot of $\\sqrt{\\nu}$ against atomic mass did not give a straight line, so option (3) fails.\nThis showed that Z is more fundamental than atomic mass. Mendeleev's law was then restated as the Modern Periodic Law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Dobereiner's triad Cl–Br–I has atomic weights Cl = 35.5, Br = 80 and I = 127. By how much does the actual atomic weight of bromine fall short of the value predicted by the Law of Triads?","image":"","options":["0.50","1.25","2.50","0.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Cl = 35.5, I = 127, and the actual value of Br = 80.\nFormula: predicted Br = (Cl + I)/2, and shortfall = predicted − actual.\nSubstituting: predicted Br = (35.5 + 127)/2 = 162.5/2 = 81.25, so shortfall = 81.25 − 80.\nSo the actual value of Br is 1.25 lower than predicted. The value 0.50 is the corresponding gap for Sr in the Ca–Sr–Ba triad, not for Br.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements are metalloids (semi-metals)?\nSi, Na, Ge, Cl, As, Mg, Te, O","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Metalloids lie along the zig-zag line that separates metals from non-metals. They show properties of both.\nMetalloids in the list: Si, Ge, As, Te, which makes 4.\nNa and Mg are metals. Cl and O are non-metals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Gallium is in group 13 and selenium is in group 16. Using the usual valences of representative elements, what is the most likely formula of the binary compound they form?","image":"","options":["GaSe","Ga$_2$Se$_3$","Ga$_3$Se$_2$","GaSe$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Gallium (group 13) has 3 valence electrons, so its valence is 3.\nSelenium (group 16) has 6 valence electrons, so its valence is 8 − 6 = 2.\nCrossing the valences, 2 Ga atoms give 2 × 3 = 6, which balances 3 Se atoms giving 3 × 2 = 6. The formula is Ga$_2$Se$_3$.\nThis matches Al$_2$S$_3$ from the same two groups.\nGa$_3$Se$_2$ is the tempting wrong choice. It comes from swapping the two valences.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Dobereiner's triad Ca, Sr, Ba, the atomic weight of strontium (88) is close to the average of the atomic weights of calcium (40) and barium (137).\nStatement II: Newlands' Law of Octaves was found to hold for all the elements known in his time.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. The mean of 40 and 137 is 88.5, which is very close to 88, the atomic weight of Sr. This is the Law of Triads.\nStatement II: Incorrect. NCERT states that the Law of Octaves seemed to be true only for elements up to calcium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly arranges Cl, F, S, O and Ne in order from the most negative to the least negative (or most positive) electron gain enthalpy?","image":"","options":["F > Cl > O > S > Ne","Cl > F > O > S > Ne","Cl > S > F > O > Ne","Cl > F > S > O > Ne"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The NCERT values are Cl = -349, F = -328, S = -200, O = -141 and Ne = +116 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nCl is more negative than F, and S is more negative than O. An electron added to the compact n = 2 shell of F or O suffers greater repulsion than one added to the larger n = 3 shell.\nHalogens are more negative than chalcogens because they gain a noble gas configuration with one electron.\nNe is positive because the added electron must enter the next shell.\nThe order is Cl > F > S > O > Ne.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The first ionization enthalpy of oxygen is lower than that of nitrogen.\nReason (R): In ionizing oxygen, the electron is removed from a 2p orbital, whereas in ionizing nitrogen it is removed from the 2s orbital.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: oxygen ($2s^2 2p^4$) has a lower first ionization enthalpy than nitrogen ($2s^2 2p^3$).\nR is false: in both atoms the electron removed comes from a 2p orbital.\nThe real reason is electron pairing. In oxygen, two of the four 2p electrons share one orbital, which increases electron-electron repulsion and makes one of them easier to remove.\nNitrogen's three 2p electrons are unpaired, one in each orbital (Hund's rule).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which scientist, a French geologist, arranged the then known elements in order of increasing atomic weight in 1862 and displayed them on a cylindrical table to show the periodic recurrence of properties?","image":"","options":["John Newlands","Johann Dobereiner","A.E.B. de Chancourtois","Lothar Meyer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: In 1862, the French geologist A.E.B. de Chancourtois made a cylindrical table of elements arranged by increasing atomic weight. His work did not attract much attention.\nNewlands was an English chemist who proposed octaves in 1865.\nDobereiner (German) proposed triads, and Lothar Meyer (German) drew property curves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which equation correctly represents the process whose enthalpy change is the first ionization enthalpy of an element X?","image":"","options":["$X(g) \\rightarrow X^{+}(g) + e^{-}$","$X(s) \\rightarrow X^{+}(g) + e^{-}$","$X(g) + e^{-} \\rightarrow X^{-}(g)$","$X(aq) \\rightarrow X^{+}(aq) + e^{-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First ionization enthalpy is the energy needed to remove the most loosely bound electron from an isolated gaseous atom in its ground state.\nThe atom must be gaseous and isolated, so neighbouring atoms, lattice energy and solvation do not affect the value.\nOption (2) starts from the solid, so its enthalpy also includes atomization.\nOption (3) is the definition of electron gain enthalpy.\nOption (4) includes hydration effects.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The general outer electronic configuration of the f-block (inner-transition) elements is:","image":"","options":["$ns^2np^{1-6}$","$(n-1)d^{1-10}ns^{0-2}$","$(n-2)f^{1-14}(n-1)d^{0-1}ns^2$","$(n-1)f^{1-14}nd^{0-1}ns^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In f-block elements the last electron enters an f-orbital two shells inside the valence shell, that is, $(n-2)f$.\nThe $(n-1)d$ subshell holds 0 or 1 electron, and $ns$ holds 2.\nSo the general configuration is $(n-2)f^{1-14}(n-1)d^{0-1}ns^2$.\nOption (4) puts the f-subshell only one shell inside, which is wrong. For example, 4f fills in period 6 ($n = 6$, so $n-2 = 4$).\nOptions (1) and (2) are the p-block and d-block configurations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the isoelectronic species N$^{3-}$, O$^{2-}$, Na$^+$, Mg$^{2+}$ and Al$^{3+}$. How many of these have an ionic radius smaller than that of F$^-$?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Every species listed has 10 electrons, so size depends only on nuclear charge. A larger Z pulls the same electrons in more strongly and gives a smaller ion.\nThe nuclear charges are N (7), O (8), F (9), Na (11), Mg (12) and Al (13).\nNa$^+$, Mg$^{2+}$ and Al$^{3+}$ have Z greater than 9, so they are smaller than F$^-$.\nN$^{3-}$ and O$^{2-}$ have Z less than 9, so they are larger than F$^-$.\nThe count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Between 1800 and 1865, the number of identified elements more than doubled, rising from 31 to 63.\nStatement II: In each of Dobereiner's triads, the properties of the middle element were in between those of the other two members.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In 1800 only 31 elements were known, and by 1865 there were 63, which is more than double. This growing number created the need for classification.\nStatement II is correct. Dobereiner noted that both the atomic weight and the properties of the middle element of each triad were intermediate between those of the other two.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Among the elements O, S, F and Cl, which has the most negative electron gain enthalpy?","image":"","options":["F","O","Cl","S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative from left to right across a period, so the group 17 elements are ahead of the group 16 elements.\nF and O are small atoms. An electron added to them enters the compact n = 2 shell and is strongly repelled by the electrons already there.\nIn Cl and S, the added electron goes into the larger n = 3 shell, where repulsion is much less.\nSo Cl (–349 kJ mol$^{-1}$) is more negative than F (–328 kJ mol$^{-1}$). Choosing F is the common trap.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (observation about first ionization enthalpy) with List-II (reason).\nList-I\n(A) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of Be > that of B\n(B) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of N > that of O\n(C) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of Ga is slightly more than that of Al\n(D) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of $^{35}Cl$ = that of $^{37}Cl$\nList-II\n(i) Same nuclear charge and same electronic configuration\n(ii) A 2s electron penetrates closer to the nucleus than a 2p electron\n(iii) Extra electron-electron repulsion from a doubly occupied 2p orbital\n(iv) Poor shielding by the intervening 3d electrons","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(ii): B loses a 2p electron, which is more shielded than the penetrating 2s electron lost by Be.\n(B)-(iii): Oxygen's fourth 2p electron is paired in an orbital, so repulsion makes it easier to remove. Nitrogen's three 2p electrons are unpaired, as Hund's rule requires.\n(C)-(iv): Ga has ten 3d electrons that shield poorly, so its effective nuclear charge is higher than expected.\n(D)-(i): Isotopes differ only in neutrons. Nuclear mass does not affect the valence electrons.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the 3d transition series in the fourth period begins with which element?","image":"","options":["Potassium (Z = 19)","Scandium (Z = 21)","Titanium (Z = 22)","Zinc (Z = 30)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In period 4, the 4s orbital fills first (K, Ca).\nBefore 4p begins, filling 3d becomes energetically favourable. This starts at scandium, Z = 21, with configuration $3d^{1}4s^{2}$.\nThe 3d orbitals become completely filled at zinc, Z = 30 ($3d^{10}4s^{2}$). So Zn ends the series rather than starting it.\nPotassium is an s-block element.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The metallic radius of sodium is 186 pm. What is the distance between the centres of two adjacent sodium atoms (metal cores) in solid sodium?","image":"","options":["372 pm","93 pm","186 pm","744 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: metallic radius of Na = 186 pm.\nFormula: metallic radius = $\\frac{1}{2}$ × internuclear distance between adjacent metal cores. So the distance d = 2r.\nSubstituting: d = 2 × 186 pm.\nSo the distance between adjacent Na cores = 372 pm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The covalent radius of chlorine is half of the Cl–Cl bond distance in $Cl_2$.\n(ii) The size of an individual atom can be measured precisely because its electron cloud has a sharp boundary.\n(iii) Among the alkali metals Li to Cs, caesium has the largest atomic radius.\n(iv) An anion is larger than its parent atom because the extra electrons increase electron–electron repulsion and lower the effective nuclear charge.\n(v) Among isoelectronic species, the one with the higher nuclear charge has the larger radius.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 198 pm ÷ 2 = 99 pm.\n(ii) Incorrect: the electron cloud has no sharp boundary, so atomic size can only be estimated from distances between atoms in the combined state.\n(iii) Correct: Cs has the largest radius at 262 pm.\n(iv) Correct: this is the NCERT reason why anions are larger.\n(v) Incorrect: a higher nuclear charge gives a smaller radius.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following processes correctly represents the change whose enthalpy is defined as the electron gain enthalpy of an element X?","image":"","options":["$X(g) \\rightarrow X^{+}(g) + e^{-}$","$X(g) + e^{-} \\rightarrow X^{-}(g)$","$X(s) + e^{-} \\rightarrow X^{-}(aq)$","$X^{-}(g) \\rightarrow X(g) + e^{-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Electron gain enthalpy, $\\Delta_{eg}H$, is the enthalpy change when an electron is added to a neutral, isolated gaseous atom to form a negative ion.\nOption (2), $X(g) + e^{-} \\rightarrow X^{-}(g)$, matches this definition exactly.\nOption (1) is ionization, not electron gain.\nOption (3) uses a solid and an aqueous ion, not isolated gaseous species.\nOption (4) is the reverse process, electron detachment from the anion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Within a period, chemical reactivity is highest for the elements at the two extremes and lowest for those in the centre.\nStatement II: Both the alkali metals and the halogens show their high reactivity mainly by losing electrons to form cations.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On the left, the ionization enthalpy is lowest. On the right (excluding noble gases), the electron gain enthalpy is most negative. So reactivity peaks at both ends and is lowest in the centre.\nStatement II is incorrect. Alkali metals react by losing an electron to form cations. Halogens react by gaining an electron to form anions, not by losing electrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the Modern Periodic Law and the long form of the Periodic Table is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["On the basis of quantum numbers, the seventh period, like the sixth, has a theoretical maximum of 32 elements.","Neptunium and plutonium, like actinium and protactinium, are found in pitchblende, an ore of uranium.","The number of elements in each period equals the number of atomic orbitals available in the energy level being filled.","The horizontal rows that Mendeleev called series are called periods in the long form."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: periods 6 and 7 fill s, f, d and p orbitals, which is 16 orbitals, so at most 32 elements.\n(2) Correct: NCERT notes that Np and Pu occur in pitchblende, so they are counted among the 94 naturally occurring elements.\n(3) Incorrect: the number of elements in a period is twice the number of orbitals being filled, because each orbital holds 2 electrons. For example, period 2 has 4 orbitals and 8 elements.\n(4) Correct: Mendeleev's series are called periods in the long form.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are numerical scales of electronegativity?\nPauling scale, Mulliken-Jaffe scale, Allred-Rochow scale, Moseley scale, Dobereiner scale","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pauling, Mulliken-Jaffe and Allred-Rochow are electronegativity scales. Of these, Pauling's is the most widely used.\nMoseley studied X-ray spectra and showed that atomic number is more fundamental than atomic mass. He did not devise an electronegativity scale.\nDobereiner proposed the Law of Triads, not an electronegativity scale.\nSo 3 of the five are electronegativity scales.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Lithium, unlike the other alkali metals, forms compounds with pronounced covalent character and resembles magnesium in many properties.\nReason (R): Lithium has a small size, a large charge/radius ratio and a relatively high electronegativity compared with the other alkali metals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Li forms covalent-type compounds, and its similarity to Mg (the second element of the next group) is called a diagonal relationship.\nR is true. NCERT attributes the anomalous behaviour of the first member of a group to its small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity.\nThese features are exactly what cause Li's covalent character, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (oxide) with List-II (nature of oxide).\nList-I:\n(A) Na$_2$O\n(B) Cl$_2$O$_7$\n(C) Al$_2$O$_3$\n(D) NO\nList-II:\n(i) Amphoteric\n(ii) Neutral\n(iii) Basic\n(iv) Acidic","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Na$_2$O is basic. It is the oxide of the extreme-left element and gives NaOH with water.\n(B) Cl$_2$O$_7$ is acidic. It is the oxide of the extreme-right element and gives HClO$_4$ with water.\n(C) Al$_2$O$_3$ is amphoteric. It belongs to an element near the centre and reacts with both acids and bases.\n(D) NO is neutral. It has no acidic or basic properties.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (radius) with List-II (value).\nList-I:\n(A) Covalent radius of Cl\n(B) Metallic radius of Cu\n(C) Ionic radius of $F^-$\n(D) Ionic radius of $Na^+$\nList-II:\n(i) 95 pm\n(ii) 99 pm\n(iii) 128 pm\n(iv) 136 pm","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Cl: half of 198 pm = 99 pm, so (ii).\n(B) Cu: half of 256 pm = 128 pm, so (iii).\n(C) $F^-$: 136 pm, much larger than the F atom's 64 pm, so (iv).\n(D) $Na^+$: 95 pm, much smaller than the Na atom's 186 pm, so (i).\nThe tempting option (2) swaps 95 and 99, but a cation must be smaller than a covalently bonded Cl radius here, according to the data.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following oxides are either amphoteric or neutral?\nNa$_2$O, Al$_2$O$_3$, CO, Cl$_2$O$_7$, As$_2$O$_3$, N$_2$O, SO$_3$, NO","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Na$_2$O comes from the far left of its period. It is basic (with water it gives NaOH).\nAl$_2$O$_3$ and As$_2$O$_3$ come from the centre of their periods. Both are amphoteric.\nCO, N$_2$O and NO are neutral. They show neither acidic nor basic properties.\nCl$_2$O$_7$ (it gives HClO$_4$ with water) and SO$_3$ come from the right side of the table. Both are acidic.\nSo the amphoteric or neutral oxides are Al$_2$O$_3$, As$_2$O$_3$, CO, N$_2$O and NO, which makes 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Moseley's observation can be written as $\\sqrt{\\nu} = a(Z - 1)$ for a particular X-ray line, where a is a constant. The frequency of this line for an unknown element is 4 times its frequency for magnesium (Z = 12). What is the atomic number of the unknown element?","image":"","options":["24","45","48","23"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu_X = 4\\nu_{Mg}$, Z(Mg) = 12, and $\\sqrt{\\nu} = a(Z-1)$.\nFormula: dividing the two relations gives $\\sqrt{\\frac{\\nu_X}{\\nu_{Mg}}} = \\frac{Z_X - 1}{Z_{Mg} - 1}$.\nSubstituting: $\\sqrt{4} = 2 = \\frac{Z_X - 1}{11}$, so $Z_X - 1 = 22$.\nSo $Z_X$ = 23.\nThe tempting value 24 comes from doubling Z directly and ignoring the '−1' term.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the Pauling electronegativity values listed in NCERT, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Carbon and sulphur have the same electronegativity","Beryllium and aluminium have the same electronegativity","Iodine and carbon have the same electronegativity","Chlorine is more electronegative than nitrogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: C is 2.5 and S is 2.5.\n(2) Correct: Be is 1.5 and Al is 1.5. This equality matches their diagonal relationship.\n(3) Correct: I is 2.5, the same as C.\n(4) Incorrect: Cl is 3.0 and N is also 3.0, so neither is more electronegative on this scale.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which d-block element is the exception to the general configuration $(n-1)d^{1-10}ns^{1-2}$, having an outer configuration of $4d^{10}5s^0$?","image":"","options":["Silver","Nickel","Platinum","Palladium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The d-block general configuration is $(n-1)d^{1-10}ns^{0-2}$. The $ns^0$ case covers palladium.\nPd has the configuration $4d^{10}5s^0$, with no electron in 5s.\nSilver is $4d^{10}5s^1$, so it does have a 5s electron.\nNickel is in the 3d series and platinum is in the 5d series, so neither has a 4d configuration.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match each block in List-I with its general outer electronic configuration in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) s-block\n(B) p-block\n(C) d-block\n(D) f-block\nList-II:\n(I) $(n-1)d^{1-10}ns^{0-2}$\n(II) $ns^2np^{1-6}$\n(III) $(n-2)f^{1-14}(n-1)d^{0-1}ns^2$\n(IV) $ns^{1-2}$","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) s-block (groups 1, 2): $ns^1$ or $ns^2$, so IV.\n(B) p-block (groups 13–18): $ns^2np^{1}$ to $ns^2np^6$, so II.\n(C) d-block (groups 3–12): an inner d subshell is being filled, $(n-1)d^{1-10}ns^{0-2}$, so I.\n(D) f-block (lanthanoids and actinoids): the last electron enters the $(n-2)f$ subshell, so III.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The energy of the electron in a hydrogen atom is $E_n = -\\frac{2.18 \\times 10^{-18}}{n^2}$ J. A sample of gaseous hydrogen atoms is entirely in the first excited state (n = 2). What energy is needed to ionize one mole of these atoms? ($N_A = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}$ $mol^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["328 kJ $mol^{-1}$","1313 kJ $mol^{-1}$","656 kJ $mol^{-1}$","164 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the electron is in n = 2, and ionization means taking it to n = $\\infty$, where E = 0.\nFormula: energy per atom = $0 - E_2 = \\frac{2.18 \\times 10^{-18}}{4}$ = $5.45 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nSubstituting for one mole: $5.45 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 6.022 \\times 10^{23}$ = $3.28 \\times 10^{5}$ J.\nSo the energy needed is about 328 kJ $mol^{-1}$, one quarter of the ground-state value (1313 kJ $mol^{-1}$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Among the halogens, which element has the most negative electron gain enthalpy?","image":"","options":["Fluorine","Chlorine","Bromine","Iodine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The NCERT values (kJ mol$^{-1}$) are F –328, Cl –349, Br –325 and I –295.\nYou might expect fluorine to be most negative because it is the smallest halogen.\nHowever, an electron added to fluorine's compact 2p subshell suffers strong repulsion from the electrons already there.\nIn chlorine, the added electron goes into the larger 3p subshell, where repulsion is much less.\nSo chlorine has the most negative value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The elements with temporary symbols Une and Uun have consecutive atomic numbers, and the sum of the atomic numbers of the elements with symbols Unq and Uub is 216.\nStatement II: The element whose systematic name was Unnilennium was later officially named Darmstadtium.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Une is un-nil-enn, which is 109. Uun is un-un-nil, which is 110. These are consecutive.\nUnq is element 104 and Uub is element 112, and 104 + 112 = 216. So Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: Unnilennium is element 109, which is Meitnerium (Mt). Darmstadtium is element 110 (Ununnillium). So Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electron gain enthalpy ($\\Delta_{eg}H$) is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Noble gases have positive $\\Delta_{eg}H$ because the added electron must enter the next higher principal shell","Oxygen has a less negative $\\Delta_{eg}H$ than sulphur","Fluorine has the most negative $\\Delta_{eg}H$ among all the elements","Within a group, $\\Delta_{eg}H$ generally becomes less negative down the group"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: for a noble gas, the extra electron would have to enter a new shell, so the process is endothermic.\n(2) Correct: an electron added to the small n = 2 level of O suffers strong repulsion. S therefore has the more negative value (−200 vs −141 kJ/mol).\n(3) Incorrect: for the same reason, Cl (−349) is more negative than F (−328). Chlorine has the most negative value.\n(4) Correct: atomic size increases down a group, so the added electron is farther from the nucleus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"A main-group element X has successive ionization enthalpies of $\\Delta_iH_1$ = 738, $\\Delta_iH_2$ = 1451 and $\\Delta_iH_3$ = 7733 kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the most likely formula of the stable binary compound formed between X and nitrogen?","image":"","options":["XN","X$_3$N","X$_3$N$_2$","X$_2$N$_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: There is a huge jump between $\\Delta_iH_2$ and $\\Delta_iH_3$ (about 5 times). So the third electron comes from a noble gas core, and X has 2 valence electrons. X is in group 2 (these values match Mg) and has valence 2.\nStep 2: Nitrogen (group 15, 5 valence electrons) has valence 8 − 5 = 3.\nStep 3: Balance the valences: 3 × 2 = 2 × 3, giving X$_3$N$_2$ (for example, Mg$_3$N$_2$).\nX$_3$N would fit a group 1 metal, which needs a jump right after $\\Delta_iH_1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The fifth period contains only 18 elements, even though the n = 5 shell can in principle hold up to 50 electrons.\nReason (R): Within the fifth period, only the 5s, 4d and 5p orbitals are filled (energy order 5s < 4d < 5p), giving 9 orbitals in all.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A is true. Period 5 runs from Rb to Xe, which is 18 elements. A shell with n = 5 could hold $2n^2 = 50$ electrons.\nStep 2: R is true. The 4f and 5d orbitals lie higher in energy and are filled only in later periods. Within period 5, electrons enter 5s (1), 4d (5) and 5p (3) orbitals, which makes 9 orbitals.\nStep 3: 9 orbitals × 2 electrons = 18 elements. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Using Mendeleev's predicted atomic weight of eka-silicon (72) and his predicted oxide formula, calculate the mass percentage of oxygen in the predicted oxide. (Atomic mass of O = 16)","image":"","options":["30.77 %","30.59 %","18.18 %","25.00 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the predicted oxide of eka-silicon is EO$_2$, with E = 72 and O = 16.\nFormula: % O = $\\frac{2 \\times 16}{72 + 2 \\times 16} \\times 100$\nSubstituting: % O = $\\frac{32}{104} \\times 100$\nSo % O = 30.77 %.\nUsing the found atomic weight of Ge (72.6) gives 30.59 %. Using the formula EO gives 18.18 %. Both are traps.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following oxides is neutral, showing neither acidic nor basic properties?","image":"","options":["$Na_2O$","$Al_2O_3$","$Cl_2O_7$","$CO$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$Na_2O$ comes from the extreme left of period 3 and is basic.\n$Cl_2O_7$ comes from the extreme right and is acidic.\n$Al_2O_3$ is amphoteric: it acts as an acid with bases and as a base with acids. That is not the same as neutral.\n$CO$, like NO and $N_2O$, is a neutral oxide.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the early development of the periodic classification is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Dobereiner was the first to consider the idea of trends among the properties of elements.","Lothar Meyer and Mendeleev, working independently, both proposed in 1869 that similarities in properties recur at regular intervals when elements are arranged by increasing atomic weight.","Unlike Newlands, Lothar Meyer observed that the length of the repeating pattern changes.","Lothar Meyer's table, developed by 1868, was published before Mendeleev's work and is therefore credited as the basis of the modern table."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT names Dobereiner as the first to consider trends among the properties of elements.\n(2) Correct: Meyer and Mendeleev worked independently and both proposed this in 1869.\n(3) Correct: Meyer noticed a change in the length of the repeating pattern, whereas Newlands assumed a fixed repeat of eight.\n(4) Incorrect: Meyer's 1868 table closely resembled the modern table, but it was not published until after Mendeleev's work. Mendeleev is the one generally credited.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electron gain enthalpy is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In Group 1, $\\Delta_{eg}H$ becomes less negative from Li to Cs.","Noble gases have large positive $\\Delta_{eg}H$ because the added electron must enter the next higher principal shell.","Electron affinity, as defined in many books, is the negative of the enthalpy change for adding an electron, so it is positive when energy is released.","$\\Delta_{eg}H$ is negative for every element, because adding an electron to an atom always releases energy."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the values change from –60 (Li) to –46 kJ mol$^{-1}$ (Cs) as atomic size increases.\n(2) Correct: this is the NCERT reason for noble gases.\n(3) Correct: electron affinity is taken as positive when energy is released, which is opposite to the thermodynamic sign convention.\n(4) Incorrect: adding an electron can be endothermic or exothermic depending on the element. Noble gases, for example, have positive values.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The 4f-inner transition series (lanthanoids), in which the 4f orbitals are progressively filled, extends from:","image":"","options":["Ce (Z = 58) to Lu (Z = 71)","La (Z = 57) to Yb (Z = 70)","Th (Z = 90) to Lr (Z = 103)","Ce (Z = 58) to Yb (Z = 70)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In period 6, electrons enter the 6s, 4f, 5d and 6p orbitals in that order.\nAs NCERT states, filling of the 4f orbitals begins with cerium (Z = 58) and ends at lutetium (Z = 71). These 14 elements form the lanthanoid series.\nTh to Lr (Z = 90 to 103) is the actinoid (5f) series.\nThe options starting at La or ending at Yb shift the series by one element.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The electronegativity of a given element is not constant; it varies with the element to which it is bonded.\nStatement II: Electronegativity increases down a group because the nuclear charge increases.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that the electronegativity of an element is not constant and depends on its bonding partner.\nStatement II is incorrect. Electronegativity decreases down a group, for example from F (4.0) to At (2.2).\nThe reason is that atomic radius increases down a group, so the valence electrons are farther from the nucleus and are held less strongly.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match the ground-state outer electronic configuration in List-I with the position in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $[Ar]3d^{10}4s^2 4p^3$\n(B) $[Kr]4d^{10}5s^0$\n(C) $[Xe]6s^1$\n(D) $[Kr]4d^1 5s^2$\nList-II\n(i) Period 5, Group 3\n(ii) Period 4, Group 15\n(iii) Period 6, Group 1\n(iv) Period 5, Group 10","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The highest shell is n = 4. The configuration $4s^2 4p^3$ has 5 valence electrons in the p-block, so it is Group 15 (arsenic) → (ii).\nB: $4d^{10}5s^0$ is the exceptional configuration of Pd. The d-electron count of 10 places it in Group 10, Period 5 → (iv).\nC: $6s^1$ is an alkali metal in Period 6 (Cs) → (iii).\nD: $4d^1 5s^2$ is the first element of the 4d series (yttrium), Group 3 → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Glenn T. Seaborg's work on the transuranium elements led to the actinoids being placed below the lanthanoids. Which element is named in his honour, and what is its atomic number?","image":"","options":["Bohrium, 107","Hassium, 108","Seaborgium, 105","Seaborgium, 106"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: NCERT states that element 106 has been named Seaborgium (Sg) in Seaborg's honour.\nStep 2: Its systematic name is Unnilhexium (Unh).\nStep 3: The most tempting wrong option gives the correct name with the wrong number. Element 105 is Dubnium (Db).\nStep 4: Bohrium is 107 and Hassium is 108.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about ionization enthalpy are correct?\n(a) It is defined for an isolated gaseous atom in its ground state.\n(b) In a plot of first ionization enthalpy against atomic number, the maxima occur at the noble gases.\n(c) Shielding of valence electrons is most effective when the inner shells are completely filled.\n(d) Removing an electron from an orbital of lower n is easier than from an orbital of higher n.\n(e) Down a group, the increase in nuclear charge outweighs the increase in shielding.\n(f) Ionization enthalpy becomes negative when the cation formed is very stable.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: this is the standard condition that makes values comparable.\n(b) Correct: closed shells are very stable, so noble gases give the peaks.\n(c) Correct: NCERT notes that shielding is effective for completely filled inner shells, as in the alkali metals.\n(d) Incorrect: lower-n electrons are closer to the nucleus and harder to remove.\n(e) Incorrect: down a group, shielding outweighs nuclear charge, so ionization enthalpy decreases.\n(f) Incorrect: energy is always needed, so ionization enthalpy is always positive.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (IUPAC systematic name) with List-II (official IUPAC name).\nList-I:\n(A) Unnilpentium\n(B) Ununbium\n(C) Ununtrium\n(D) Ununquadium\nList-II:\n(I) Nihonium\n(II) Flerovium\n(III) Dubnium\n(IV) Copernicium","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Unnilpentium is element 105, which is Dubnium (III).\n(B) Ununbium is element 112, which is Copernicium (IV).\n(C) Ununtrium is element 113, which is Nihonium (I).\n(D) Ununquadium is element 114, which is Flerovium (II).\nSo the correct matching is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements have a positive electron gain enthalpy?\nHe, Li, Ne, F, Ar, O, Kr, Na","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Positive $\\Delta_{eg}H$ values occur for the noble gases, because the added electron must enter a new shell:\n- He: +48 kJ mol$^{-1}$\n- Ne: +116 kJ mol$^{-1}$\n- Ar: +96 kJ mol$^{-1}$\n- Kr: +96 kJ mol$^{-1}$\nThe other elements release energy on gaining an electron: Li (–60), F (–328), O (–141) and Na (–53 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nSo 4 elements have a positive value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (oxide) with List-II (nature of oxide).\nList-I:\n(A) $Na_2O$\n(B) $Cl_2O_7$\n(C) $As_2O_3$\n(D) $N_2O$\nList-II:\n(I) Amphoteric\n(II) Neutral\n(III) Basic\n(IV) Acidic","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $Na_2O$ is basic (III). With water it gives NaOH, a strong base.\n(B) $Cl_2O_7$ is acidic (IV). With water it gives $HClO_4$, a strong acid.\n(C) $As_2O_3$ is amphoteric (I). It comes from an element near the centre of the table, like $Al_2O_3$.\n(D) $N_2O$ is neutral (II), like CO and NO.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Use the following radii: F atom = 64 pm, $F^-$ = 136 pm, Na atom = 186 pm, $Na^+$ = 95 pm. Calculate the ratio of the percentage increase in radius when F forms $F^-$ to the percentage decrease in radius when Na forms $Na^+$.","image":"","options":["2.30","0.43","1.43","2.10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 64 pm, $F^-$ = 136 pm, Na = 186 pm, $Na^+$ = 95 pm.\nFormula: % change = $\\frac{|\\text{change}|}{\\text{atomic radius}}$ × 100.\nSubstituting for F → $F^-$: (136 − 64)/64 × 100 = 112.5%.\nSubstituting for Na → $Na^+$: (186 − 95)/186 × 100 ≈ 48.9%.\nRatio = 112.5/48.9 ≈ 2.30.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In 1913 Henry Moseley studied the characteristic X-ray spectra of elements. Which plot did he find to be a straight line? ($\\nu$ = frequency of the X-rays emitted)","image":"","options":["$\\nu$ against atomic mass","$\\sqrt{\\nu}$ against atomic number (Z)","$\\nu^2$ against atomic number (Z)","$\\sqrt{\\nu}$ against atomic mass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Moseley plotted $\\sqrt{\\nu}$ of the characteristic X-rays against atomic number Z and got a straight line.\nThe same quantity, $\\sqrt{\\nu}$, plotted against atomic mass did not give a straight line.\nThis showed that atomic number is a more fundamental property of an element than its atomic mass.\nOption (4) is the most tempting wrong choice because it uses the correct quantity, $\\sqrt{\\nu}$, but the wrong variable.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Group 13 elements do not show a smooth decrease in first ionization enthalpy down the group. This is because Ga follows the first d-series, and Tl follows both the 4f and 5d series. Which order correctly shows decreasing first ionization enthalpy?","image":"","options":["B > Tl > Ga > Al > In","B > Al > Ga > In > Tl","B > Ga > Al > Tl > In","B > Tl > Al > Ga > In"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Values (kJ $mol^{-1}$): B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589.\nd and f electrons shield the nuclear charge poorly. Ga (after 3d) and Tl (after 4f and 5d) therefore feel a higher effective nuclear charge than expected.\nAs a result, Ga is slightly above Al, and Tl rises above both Ga and In.\nThe decreasing order is B > Tl > Ga > Al > In.\nOption (2) is the 'expected' smooth trend, which the data contradict.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"According to the Periodic Law as first published by Mendeleev, the properties of elements are a periodic function of their:","image":"","options":["atomic numbers","atomic weights","numbers of valence electrons","atomic volumes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mendeleev stated that the properties of elements are a periodic function of their atomic weights.\nHe arranged elements in rows and columns in order of increasing atomic weight.\nAtomic number became the basis only after Moseley's X-ray work in 1913. That change gave the Modern Periodic Law.\nAtomic volume is a property that Lothar Meyer plotted against atomic weight. It was not the basis of the law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The first ionization enthalpy of sodium is 496 kJ mol$^{-1}$. How much energy is required to convert all the atoms in 2.3 mg of sodium vapour into gaseous $Na^{+}$ ions? (Atomic mass of Na = 23 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["4.96 J","49.6 J","496 J","0.496 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: mass = 2.3 mg = $2.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ g; $\\Delta_i H$ = 496 kJ mol$^{-1}$; M = 23 g mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: energy = moles × $\\Delta_i H$, where moles = mass / M.\nSubstituting: moles = $2.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ / 23 = $1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol.\nEnergy = $1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ × 496000 J mol$^{-1}$ = 49.6 J.\nSo the energy required = 49.6 J.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The bond distance in a bromine molecule ($Br_2$) is 228 pm. What is the covalent radius of bromine?","image":"","options":["114 pm","228 pm","57 pm","456 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Br–Br single-bond distance in $Br_2$ = 228 pm.\nFormula: covalent radius = $\\frac{1}{2}$ × internuclear distance between two identical atoms joined by a single covalent bond.\nSubstituting: $r = \\frac{228}{2}$ pm.\nSo the covalent radius of Br = 114 pm, which matches the value in Table 3.6(b).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although helium strictly belongs to the s-block, it is placed in group 18 along with the p-block noble gases.\nReason (R): Helium has a completely filled valence shell (1s$^2$) and shows properties characteristic of the other noble gases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The last electron of helium enters the 1s orbital, which makes it an s-block element. Even so, it is placed in group 18.\nR is true. The 1s$^2$ configuration is a closed valence shell, so helium is very unreactive like the other noble gases.\nR is exactly the justification NCERT gives for this placement.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Glenn T. Seaborg discovered plutonium and worked on the transuranium elements from 94 to 102. Which option correctly states a consequence of his work and the element named in his honour?","image":"","options":["Lanthanoids were moved into group 3 of the main table; element 104","Transuranium elements were placed in group 18; element 101","Actinoids were placed in the same period as lanthanoids; element 106","Actinoids were placed below the lanthanoids; element 106 (Seaborgium, Sg)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Seaborg's work on elements 94 to 102 led to the periodic table being reconfigured, with the actinoids placed below the lanthanoids.\nHe received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1951.\nElement 106 is named Seaborgium (Sg) in his honour.\nElement 104 is Rutherfordium and element 101 is Mendelevium, so options (1) and (2) are wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"An element has the ground-state outer electronic configuration $[Kr]\\,4d^{10}5s^{2}5p^{3}$. Its position in the long form of the Periodic Table is:","image":"","options":["Period 4, Group 15","Period 5, Group 15","Period 5, Group 13","Period 4, Group 5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The period number equals the principal quantum number n of the valence shell. Here the outermost shell is n = 5, so the element is in period 5.\nThe valence configuration is $5s^{2}5p^{3}$. It is a p-block element with 2 + 3 = 5 outer electrons, so its group is 10 + 5 = 15.\nPeriod 4 is wrong because the 4d electrons belong to the inner shell. Group 13 would need $ns^2np^1$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the elements Si, Al, Ca, K and Cs in increasing order of metallic character.","image":"","options":["Si < Al < Ca < K < Cs","Al < Si < Ca < Cs < K","Si < Al < K < Ca < Cs","Cs < K < Ca < Al < Si"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Metallic character decreases from left to right across a period and increases down a group.\nPeriod 3: Si is less metallic than Al.\nCa (period 4, group 2) lies below and to the left of Al, so Ca is more metallic than Al.\nK lies to the left of Ca in period 4, so K is more metallic than Ca.\nCs lies below K in group 1, so Cs is the most metallic.\nOrder: Si < Al < Ca < K < Cs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The electron gain enthalpy of fluorine is less negative than that of chlorine.\nReason (R): The electron added to fluorine enters the compact n = 2 shell and suffers greater electron-electron repulsion than an electron added to the larger n = 3 shell of chlorine.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A is true. The values are F = −328 kJ/mol and Cl = −349 kJ/mol, so Cl has the more negative electron gain enthalpy.\nStep 2: R is true. The n = 2 level is small, so an incoming electron is strongly repelled by the electrons already present there.\nStep 3: In the n = 3 level of Cl, the added electron spreads over a larger region, so repulsion is much less.\nStep 4: This repulsion is exactly why F is the exception to the trend, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (observed fact) with List-II (main reason).\nList-I\n(a) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of Be > $\\Delta_i H_1$ of B\n(b) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of N > $\\Delta_i H_1$ of O\n(c) $\\Delta_i H_1$ of Li > $\\Delta_i H_1$ of Na\n(d) $\\Delta_i H_2$ of Na > $\\Delta_i H_2$ of Mg\nList-II\n(i) Electron is removed from a noble-gas core\n(ii) Repulsion between paired electrons in a 2p orbital\n(iii) Greater penetration of a 2s electron than a 2p electron\n(iv) Valence electron is farther from the nucleus and more shielded","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): Be loses a 2s electron, which penetrates closer to the nucleus. B loses a more shielded 2p electron.\n(b)-(ii): Oxygen has one paired 2p orbital, and the extra repulsion lowers its ionization enthalpy.\n(c)-(iv): Down the group, the valence electron of Na is in a higher shell and is more shielded.\n(d)-(i): $Na^{+}$ has a neon core, so its second electron comes from a closed shell. $Mg^{+}$ still has a 3s electron to lose.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"All alkali metals have an $ns^{1}$ valence configuration. Which of the following is the correct ground-state configuration of caesium (Z = 55)?","image":"","options":["$[Xe]\\,6s^{1}$","$[Kr]\\,5s^{1}$","$[Xe]\\,5d^{1}$","$[Xe]\\,4f^{1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Caesium is the group 1 element of period 6, so its valence shell is n = 6.\nThe preceding noble gas is xenon (Z = 54). The 55th electron enters 6s, giving $[Xe]6s^{1}$.\n$[Kr]5s^{1}$ is rubidium (Z = 37).\nThe 5d and 4f orbitals fill only after 6s, so a single electron would not go there.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly arranges the isoelectronic species $N^{3-}$, $O^{2-}$, $F^-$, $Na^+$, $Mg^{2+}$ and $Al^{3+}$ in increasing order of ionic radius?","image":"","options":["$N^{3-} < O^{2-} < F^- < Na^+ < Mg^{2+} < Al^{3+}$","$Al^{3+} < Mg^{2+} < Na^+ < F^- < O^{2-} < N^{3-}$","$Na^+ < Mg^{2+} < Al^{3+} < N^{3-} < O^{2-} < F^-$","$F^- < O^{2-} < N^{3-} < Al^{3+} < Mg^{2+} < Na^+$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"All six species have 10 electrons, so the deciding factor is the nuclear charge Z: N = 7, O = 8, F = 9, Na = 11, Mg = 12, Al = 13.\nThe larger the Z, the stronger the pull on the same 10 electrons, and the smaller the ion.\nSo $Al^{3+}$ is the smallest and $N^{3-}$ is the largest.\nOption (1) is exactly the reverse of this order.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The 1984 IUPAC recommendation numbers the groups 1 to 18 and replaces the older notation used in Fig. 3.2. Match each old group label (List-I) with its IUPAC group number (List-II).\nList-I:\n(a) IIIB\n(b) IB\n(c) VA\n(d) 0\nList-II:\n(i) 18\n(ii) 15\n(iii) 11\n(iv) 3","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The old labels run IA, IIA, IIIB to VIIB, VIII (three columns), IB, IIB, IIIA to VIIA, 0.\n(a) IIIB is the first d-block column, which is group 3.\n(b) IB comes just after the three VIII columns (groups 8 to 10), which is group 11.\n(c) VA is the nitrogen family, which is group 15.\n(d) 0 is the noble gases, which is group 18.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following elements are representative (main group) elements?\nNa, Ca, Fe, Al, Cl, Ce, Zn, S","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Representative elements are the s-block and p-block elements together.\ns-block: Na (group 1) and Ca (group 2). These count.\np-block: Al (group 13), S (group 16) and Cl (group 17). These count.\nFe and Zn are d-block elements, and Ce is an f-block lanthanoid. These do not count.\nTotal = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about ionization enthalpy is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Ionization enthalpies are always positive because energy must always be supplied to remove an electron.","The second ionization enthalpy of an element is higher than its first ionization enthalpy.","In a plot of first ionization enthalpy against atomic number, maxima occur at noble gases and minima at alkali metals.","The term 'ionization enthalpy', when not qualified, means the total energy needed to remove all the valence electrons of an atom."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: removing an electron always requires energy, so $\\Delta_i H$ is always positive.\n(2) Correct: it is harder to pull an electron away from a positive ion than from a neutral atom.\n(3) Correct: noble gases have closed, very stable shells, while alkali metals have a single, loosely held $ns^1$ electron.\n(4) Incorrect: an unqualified 'ionization enthalpy' refers only to the first ionization enthalpy.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Until the discovery of a new element is proved and its name is officially recognised, IUPAC recommends a systematic name derived directly from its atomic number.\nReason (R): The systematic name given from the digit roots becomes the permanent name of the element and is never replaced.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. To avoid rival discovery claims, such as the dispute over element 104, IUPAC recommended temporary systematic names built from the numerical roots of the atomic number.\nR is false. The systematic name is only temporary. A permanent name and symbol are later chosen by a vote of IUPAC representatives. For example, Unnilquadium became Rutherfordium.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The Modern Periodic Law states that the physical and chemical properties of the elements are periodic functions of their:","image":"","options":["atomic weights","number of neutrons","atomic numbers","atomic volumes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mendeleev's law was based on atomic weights.\nMoseley's work showed that atomic number is more fundamental, so the law was modified.\nThe Modern Periodic Law: the properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers.\nAtomic weight (option 1) belongs to Mendeleev's original law, not the modern one.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (atomic number) with List-II (position in the Periodic Table).\nList-I:\n(A) Z = 114\n(B) Z = 117\n(C) Z = 120\n(D) Z = 87\nList-II:\n(p) Period 7, Group 1\n(q) Period 8, Group 2\n(r) Period 7, Group 14\n(s) Period 7, Group 17","image":"","options":["A-r, B-s, C-q, D-p","A-s, B-r, C-p, D-q","A-r, B-s, C-p, D-q","A-q, B-s, C-r, D-p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Z = 114 is $[Rn]5f^{14}6d^{10}7s^{2}7p^{2}$. It has 4 outer electrons in the p-block, so it is in period 7, group 14 (r).\n(B) Z = 117 is $[Rn]5f^{14}6d^{10}7s^{2}7p^{5}$. It belongs to the halogen family, period 7, group 17 (s).\n(C) Z = 120 is $[Og]8s^{2}$. It is an alkaline earth metal in period 8, group 2 (q).\n(D) Z = 87 is francium, $[Rn]7s^{1}$, in period 7, group 1 (p).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The electron gain enthalpy and the electron affinity ($A_e$, defined at absolute zero) are related by $\\Delta_{eg}H = -A_e - \\frac{5}{2}RT$. The electron gain enthalpy of chlorine at 298 K is $-349$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. Taking R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, what is the electron affinity of chlorine?","image":"","options":["342.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$","355.2 kJ mol$^{-1}$","349.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-342.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_{eg}H$ = –349 kJ mol$^{-1}$, T = 298 K, R = 8.314 J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$.\nFormula: rearranging gives $A_e = -\\Delta_{eg}H - \\frac{5}{2}RT$.\nCorrection term: $\\frac{5}{2}RT$ = 2.5 × 8.314 × 298 = 6194 J mol$^{-1}$ ≈ 6.19 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nSubstituting: $A_e$ = 349 – 6.19 = 342.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThe value is positive because energy is released when the electron is added. Option (4) has the wrong sign, and option (3) ignores the RT correction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the anomalous behaviour of second-period elements are correct?\n(i) The maximum covalency of the first member of each group in the p-block is 4.\n(ii) Carbon forms $p\\pi-p\\pi$ multiple bonds, such as C=C and C≡C, more readily than silicon does.\n(iii) Beryllium, like the other alkaline earth metals, forms predominantly ionic compounds.\n(iv) The anomalous behaviour of the first member of a group arises mainly from its large atomic size.\n(v) Aluminium can accommodate more than four electron pairs in its valence shell, while boron cannot.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Only the 2s and 2p orbitals (four in all) are available for bonding.\n(ii) Correct. First members show a greater ability to form $p\\pi-p\\pi$ multiple bonds.\n(iii) Incorrect. Be forms compounds with pronounced covalent character.\n(iv) Incorrect. The cause is small size, not large size.\n(v) Correct. Al uses 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals and forms $[AlF_6]^{3-}$; boron cannot.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"American and Soviet scientists both claimed credit for discovering one element. The Americans named it Rutherfordium and the Soviets named it Kurchatovium. What is the atomic number of this element?","image":"","options":["104","105","106","110"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: NCERT cites element 104 as the example of a disputed discovery claim between the American and Soviet groups.\nStep 2: Its official name today is Rutherfordium (Rf). Its systematic name is Unnilquadium (Unq).\nStep 3: Disputes like this led IUPAC to recommend systematic names based on atomic number until a discovery is proved.\nStep 4: Element 105 is Dubnium, 106 is Seaborgium and 110 is Darmstadtium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electron gain enthalpy ($\\Delta_{eg}H$) is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\Delta_{eg}H$ is negative for every element, because adding an electron always releases energy","When energy is released on adding an electron to a gaseous atom, $\\Delta_{eg}H$ is negative while the electron affinity is taken as positive","Noble gases have large negative $\\Delta_{eg}H$ values because their valence shells are completely filled","$\\Delta_{eg}H$ becomes more negative on moving down a group because the atomic size increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the process can be exothermic or endothermic. Noble gases have positive values.\n(2) Correct: electron affinity is defined as the negative of $\\Delta_{eg}H$. A release of energy therefore gives a negative $\\Delta_{eg}H$ but a positive electron affinity.\n(3) Incorrect: noble gases have large positive values, because the added electron must enter the next higher shell.\n(4) Incorrect: a larger atom holds the added electron farther from the nucleus, so $\\Delta_{eg}H$ generally becomes less negative down a group.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Boron forms $[BF_4]^-$ but not $[BF_6]^{3-}$, whereas aluminium forms $[AlF_6]^{3-}$. What is the main reason for this?","image":"","options":["Boron is less electronegative than aluminium","Boron has only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p) available for bonding","Boron has a larger atomic radius than aluminium","Boron cannot show an oxidation state of +3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A second-period element has only one 2s and three 2p orbitals for bonding. This limits its maximum covalency to 4, so boron stops at $[BF_4]^-$.\nAluminium has 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals (nine in all), so it can expand its valence shell and form $[AlF_6]^{3-}$.\nThe other options are false: boron is smaller and more electronegative than Al, and it does show +3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"P, Q, R and S are second-period elements with Pauling electronegativities 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 and 4.0, respectively. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) P has the largest atomic radius among the four.\n(ii) S has the highest non-metallic character among the four.\n(iii) Metallic character follows the order P > Q > R > S.\n(iv) The oxides of R are basic in nature.\n(v) P forms compounds with pronounced covalent character, unlike the other members of its group.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The values identify P = Li, Q = B, R = N and S = F.\n(i) Correct: atomic radius decreases across the period, so Li is the largest.\n(ii) Correct: electronegativity is directly related to non-metallic character, so F has the most.\n(iii) Correct: metallic character is inversely related to electronegativity.\n(iv) Incorrect: nitrogen is a non-metal, and its oxides such as $N_2O_5$ are acidic.\n(v) Correct: Li shows anomalous covalent behaviour.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The electron gain enthalpy of sulphur is more negative than that of oxygen.\nReason (R): An electron added to the small n = 2 shell of oxygen experiences greater electron–electron repulsion than one added to the larger n = 3 shell of sulphur.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $\\Delta_{eg}H$ of S is –200 kJ mol$^{-1}$, while that of O is –141 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nR is true: the n = 2 level is compact, so an incoming electron is strongly repelled by the electrons already present. In the n = 3 level, the electron occupies a larger region of space and the repulsion is much less.\nThis smaller repulsion is exactly why sulphur releases more energy than oxygen, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following elements has a positive electron gain enthalpy?","image":"","options":["Fluorine","Oxygen","Sodium","Neon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Noble gases have a completely filled valence shell. An added electron must enter the next higher principal quantum level, which gives an unstable configuration. Energy must therefore be supplied, so $\\Delta_{eg}H$ is positive. For Ne it is +116 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nF (–328), O (–141) and Na (–53 kJ mol$^{-1}$) all release energy when they gain an electron.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Mendeleev placed iodine in Group VII with fluorine, chlorine and bromine, even though its atomic weight is lower than that of tellurium. What was his main reason?","image":"","options":["He gave more importance to similar properties than to strict atomic-weight order, suspecting the atomic weights might be wrong","He arranged elements by atomic number, and iodine has the higher atomic number","Iodine and tellurium had the same atomic weight according to the data available then","Moseley's X-ray work had shown that iodine must follow tellurium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mendeleev's main aim was to keep elements with similar properties in the same group.\nIodine resembles F, Cl and Br, so he placed it in Group VII. He set aside the atomic-weight order, thinking the measurements might be incorrect.\nOption (2) is tempting, but atomic number was not known in his time. Moseley's work came only in 1913, which also rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Zn, Cd and Hg do not show most of the characteristic properties of transition elements.\nReason (R): In their ground state, Zn, Cd and Hg have partially filled $(n-1)d$ orbitals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that Zn, Cd and Hg do not show most transition properties, such as coloured ions, variable valence and paramagnetism.\nR is false. Their configuration is $(n-1)d^{10}ns^2$, so the d-subshell is completely filled, not partially filled.\nThe filled $d^{10}$ shell is actually the reason they behave differently from typical transition metals.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"What are the oxidation state and the covalency of aluminium, respectively, in the complex ion $[AlCl(H_2O)_5]^{2+}$?","image":"","options":["+2 and 6","+3 and 6","+3 and 3","+6 and 6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oxidation state: let it be x. Then $x + (-1) + 5(0) = +2$, so $x = +3$.\nCovalency: Al is bonded to one Cl and five $H_2O$ ligands, giving 6 bonds. So the covalency is 6.\nThis shows that oxidation state and covalency need not be equal.\n'+2' wrongly takes the overall ion charge as the oxidation state, and '+6' wrongly equates oxidation state with covalency.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The second ionization enthalpy of any element is higher than its first ionization enthalpy.\nReason (R): It is more difficult to remove an electron from a positively charged ion than from a neutral atom.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: successive ionization enthalpies always increase, so $\\Delta_i H_2 > \\Delta_i H_1$.\nR is true: after the first electron leaves, the remaining electrons are held by a net positive charge. Each remaining electron feels a greater attraction, so more energy is needed to remove it.\nThis greater attraction is exactly why the second value is higher, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The electron gain enthalpy of chlorine is more negative than that of fluorine.\nStatement II: Noble gases have positive electron gain enthalpies because the added electron must enter the next higher principal quantum level.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The values are Cl = –349 and F = –328 kJ mol$^{-1}$. An electron added to fluorine's small n = 2 shell faces strong repulsion from the electrons already there. In chlorine's larger n = 3 shell, this repulsion is much less.\nStatement II is correct. In a noble gas the electron must enter a new, higher shell. This gives an unstable configuration, so the value is positive (for example, Ne = +116 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The periods of the long form of the Periodic Table contain 2, 8, 8, 18, 18 and 32 elements respectively, up to period 6. What is the atomic number of the noble gas that completes the sixth period?","image":"","options":["54","86","118","80"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The noble gas at the end of a period has Z equal to the total number of elements up to and including that period.\nAdding periods 1 to 6: 2 + 8 + 8 + 18 + 18 + 32 = 86.\nZ = 86 is radon.\n54 (xenon) completes only period 5, and 118 completes period 7.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In his periodic table, Mendeleev placed iodine in Group VII, after tellurium, even though iodine has a lower atomic weight than tellurium. What was his main basis for doing this?","image":"","options":["Iodine resembles fluorine, chlorine and bromine in its properties.","Iodine has a higher atomic number than tellurium.","Iodine has a higher melting point than tellurium.","Iodine has seven electrons in its valence shell."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mendeleev's law: properties are a periodic function of atomic weights, but his main aim was to keep elements with similar properties in the same group.\nIodine behaves like F, Cl and Br. So he ignored the weight order and put it in Group VII. He suspected the atomic weight measurements might be wrong.\nOptions (2) and (4) are tempting, but atomic number and electronic structure were unknown to Mendeleev. Atomic number became the basis only after Moseley's work in 1913.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Using the valences of the elements from their groups, what is the formula of the stable binary compound formed by magnesium and nitrogen?","image":"","options":["$MgN$","$Mg_2N_3$","$Mg_3N_2$","$MgN_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mg is in group 2, so its valence is 2.\nN is in group 15. Its valence is $8 - 5 = 3$.\nCross-multiplying the valences gives $Mg_3N_2$, with total charges $3 \\times (+2) = +6$ and $2 \\times (-3) = -6$.\n$Mg_2N_3$ swaps the valences and would not be neutral.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"According to the IUPAC system for naming elements with atomic number above 100, which temporary name and symbol belong to the element with Z = 119?","image":"","options":["Ununennium, Une","Ununennium, Uue","Unennunium, Ueu","Ununoctium, Uuo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The digit roots are 1 = un (u) and 9 = enn (e).\nZ = 119 is read digit by digit as 1-1-9, which gives un + un + enn. Adding 'ium' gives ununennium.\nThe symbol uses the first letter of each root in the same order: U-u-e, so it is Uue.\n'Une' is the temporary symbol of element 109 (unnilennium), so option (1) has the right name but the wrong symbol.\nUuo stands for 118, and 'Unennunium' puts the digits in the wrong order.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution).\nList-I:\n(a) Dobereiner\n(b) de Chancourtois\n(c) Newlands\n(d) Lothar Meyer\nList-II:\n(i) Curves of atomic volume against atomic weight\n(ii) Groups of three elements with similar properties\n(iii) Cylindrical arrangement of elements\n(iv) Every eighth element resembles the first","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Dobereiner proposed triads in 1829, so (a) matches (ii).\n(b) de Chancourtois made a cylindrical table in 1862, so (b) matches (iii).\n(c) Newlands proposed the Law of Octaves in 1865, so (c) matches (iv).\n(d) Lothar Meyer plotted atomic volume and other properties against atomic weight, so (d) matches (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Lithium and beryllium form compounds with pronounced covalent character, unlike the other members of their groups.\nReason (R): Second period elements have only four valence orbitals (2s and 2p), so their maximum covalency is 4.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes the covalent character of Li and Be compounds, while other alkali and alkaline earth metals form mainly ionic compounds.\nR is true. Second period elements lack d orbitals, so their covalency is limited to 4 (for example, BF$_4^-$).\nR does not explain A. The covalent character comes from small size, large charge/radius ratio and high electronegativity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"What is the IUPAC systematic (temporary) name of the element with atomic number 107?","image":"","options":["Unnilhexium","Unnilseptium","Ununseptium","Unniloctium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Write the digits of 107 in order: 1, 0 and 7.\nStep 2: The digit roots are 1 = un, 0 = nil and 7 = sept, giving un + nil + sept.\nStep 3: Adding 'ium' gives Unnilseptium, with symbol Uns. Its official name is Bohrium (Bh).\nStep 4: Ununseptium is element 117, Unnilhexium is 106 and Unniloctium is 108.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Helium, though strictly an s-block element, is placed in group 18 because its valence shell ($1s^2$) is completely filled and it behaves like the other noble gases.\nStatement II: Hydrogen is placed separately at the top of the periodic table because it can resemble both the alkali metals and the halogens.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. He has the configuration $1s^2$, which is s-type. Its filled valence shell gives it noble-gas properties, so it is placed with group 18.\nStatement II is correct. H has one s-electron, like group 1 metals. It can also gain one electron to reach a noble gas configuration, like group 17 halogens. Being a special case, it is placed separately.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match the IUPAC temporary names in List-I with the official names in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Unnilhexium\n(b) Ununnillium\n(c) Ununquadium\n(d) Ununseptium\nList-II\n(i) Flerovium\n(ii) Tennessine\n(iii) Seaborgium\n(iv) Darmstadtium","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First convert each temporary name into its atomic number using the digit roots, then recall the official name.\n(a) Un-nil-hex = 106, which is Seaborgium (Sg), named after Glenn T. Seaborg: (iii).\n(b) Un-un-nil = 110, which is Darmstadtium (Ds): (iv).\n(c) Un-un-quad = 114, which is Flerovium (Fl): (i).\n(d) Un-un-sept = 117, which is Tennessine (Ts): (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following official name – symbol pairs are correct?\nBohrium – Bo\nLivermorium – Lr\nNobelium – No\nCopernicium – Cn\nFlerovium – Fv\nTennessine – Ts","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bohrium – Bo is incorrect. The symbol is Bh.\nLivermorium – Lr is incorrect. Lr is Lawrencium; Livermorium is Lv.\nNobelium – No is correct.\nCopernicium – Cn is correct.\nFlerovium – Fv is incorrect. The symbol is Fl.\nTennessine – Ts is correct.\nSo 3 pairs are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ionic radius of $F^-$ (136 pm) is more than double the atomic radius of fluorine (64 pm).\nReason (R): Adding an electron to an atom increases electron–electron repulsion and lowers the effective nuclear charge per electron, so the electron cloud expands.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the NCERT values are $F^-$ = 136 pm and F = 64 pm, and 136 is more than 2 × 64.\nR is true: an anion has extra electrons but the same nuclear charge. Repulsion among the electrons increases and the effective nuclear charge decreases.\nThis expansion of the electron cloud is exactly why the anion is larger than its parent atom, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match the scientist in List-I with the contribution in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Johann Dobereiner\n(B) A.E.B. de Chancourtois\n(C) John Newlands\n(D) Lothar Meyer\nList-II\n(i) Plotted atomic volume, melting point and boiling point against atomic weight\n(ii) Groups of three elements in which the middle member's atomic weight is about the average of the other two\n(iii) Cylindrical table of elements arranged by increasing atomic weight (1862)\n(iv) Every eighth element resembles the first, like notes in music","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Dobereiner proposed triads such as Li-Na-K. In each triad the middle atomic weight is close to the mean of the other two, so A-(ii).\nB: The French geologist de Chancourtois made a cylindrical table in 1862, so B-(iii).\nC: Newlands proposed the Law of Octaves, in which every eighth element is similar to the first, so C-(iv).\nD: Lothar Meyer plotted atomic volume and other physical properties against atomic weight and obtained periodic curves, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the correct decreasing order of size for the species Na, Mg, $F^-$, $Na^+$ and $Mg^{2+}$?","image":"","options":["$Na > Mg > F^- > Na^+ > Mg^{2+}$","$Na > F^- > Mg > Na^+ > Mg^{2+}$","$F^- > Na > Mg > Mg^{2+} > Na^+$","$Na > Mg > Na^+ > F^- > Mg^{2+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Na (186 pm) and Mg (160 pm) are neutral atoms whose valence electrons lie in the n = 3 shell. Na is larger because size decreases across a period.\n$F^-$, $Na^+$ and $Mg^{2+}$ each have 10 electrons, with the outermost shell n = 2. All three are therefore smaller than Na and Mg. For example, $F^-$ is 136 pm.\nWithin this isoelectronic set, the radius falls as Z rises (9, 11, 12): $F^- > Na^+ > Mg^{2+}$ (72 pm).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The first ionization enthalpy generally increases from lithium to neon across the second period. The best explanation for this trend is that:","image":"","options":["the shielding by inner-core electrons increases sharply across the period","the atomic size increases across the period","the increase in nuclear charge outweighs the small increase in shielding, so effective nuclear charge rises","each successive electron enters a new principal shell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Across a period, electrons are added to the same principal shell (n = 2).\nThe inner core (1s²) stays the same, so shielding barely increases while the nuclear charge rises by one unit at each step.\nThe effective nuclear charge therefore increases, and outer electrons are held more tightly.\nOption (1) is wrong because core shielding does not increase much across a period.\nOptions (2) and (4) are wrong because size decreases and no new shell is started.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"An element has the outer electronic configuration $(n-2)f^7(n-1)d^1ns^2$ with $n = 7$. Which statement about this element is correct?","image":"","options":["Its atomic number is 96; it is a transuranium element belonging to the actinoid series.","Its atomic number is 64; it is a lanthanoid.","Its atomic number is 96; it is a non-radioactive inner-transition metal.","Its atomic number is 94; it is a d-block element because it has a $6d^1$ electron."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"With $n = 7$, the configuration is $[Rn]5f^7 6d^1 7s^2$.\nZ = 86 + 7 + 1 + 2 = 96.\nThe 5f series makes it an actinoid. Since 96 > 92, it is a transuranium element.\nOption (2) has Z = 64, which applies only for $n = 6$ (a 4f lanthanoid).\nOption (3) fails because all actinoids are radioactive.\nOption (4) fails because the last electron is counted in 5f, so the element is f-block.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The element with the temporary IUPAC name Ununhexium is officially known as ______ and is placed in group ______ of the periodic table.","image":"","options":["Moscovium; 15","Flerovium; 14","Livermorium; 14","Livermorium; 16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: Ununhexium is un-un-hex, which gives Z = 116.\nStep 2: Element 116 is officially Livermorium (Lv).\nStep 3: Period 7 ends at Z = 118 in group 18. Counting back two places puts 116 in group 16, below polonium.\nStep 4: The option 'Livermorium; 14' has the right name but the wrong group. Flerovium (114) is in group 14 and Moscovium (115) is in group 15.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Lothar Meyer obtained a periodically repeated pattern by plotting which properties of elements against their atomic weights?","image":"","options":["Atomic volume, melting point and boiling point","Ionization enthalpy, electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity","Valence, density and atomic number","Formulae of oxides and chlorides"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: Lothar Meyer plotted physical properties such as atomic volume, melting point and boiling point against atomic weight. He obtained a periodically repeated pattern.\nIonization enthalpy and electronegativity belong to later atomic theory, not to Meyer's 1860s work.\nRelying on the formulae of oxides and chlorides was characteristic of Mendeleev.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the long form of the Periodic Table, the 14 lanthanoids and 14 actinoids are placed in separate panels at the bottom.\nReason (R): Placing them separately maintains the structure of the table and keeps elements with similar properties in a single column.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the 4f series of period 6 (lanthanoids) and the 5f series of period 7 (actinoids) are shown in separate panels below the main table.\nR is true: NCERT states this is done to maintain the structure of the table and to preserve classification by keeping similar elements in one column.\nR therefore directly explains why A is done.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Why are the atomic radii of noble gases usually left out when atomic radii across a period are compared?","image":"","options":["Noble gases are monoatomic, so their radii are non-bonded (van der Waals) radii, which are much larger and should be compared only with van der Waals radii of other elements","Noble gases have the smallest radii in their period, so they distort the trend","Noble gas atoms cannot be studied by X-ray methods","Noble gases have the same radius as the halogen just before them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that noble gases are monoatomic, so their radii are non-bonded radii.\nThese values are very large and are not covalent radii.\nThey should therefore be compared with the van der Waals radii of other elements, not with covalent radii.\nOption (2) is the tempting wrong choice. Noble gas radii are actually very large, not the smallest in the period.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (series of elements) with List-II (atomic number of the first member of the series, as given in NCERT).\nList-I:\n(A) 3d transition series\n(B) 4d transition series\n(C) Lanthanoid (4f) series\n(D) Actinoid (5f) series\nList-II:\n(I) 39\n(II) 90\n(III) 21\n(IV) 58","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The 3d series starts at scandium, $Z = 21$ ($3d^1 4s^2$), so A matches III.\n(B) The 4d series in period 5 starts at yttrium, $Z = 39$, so B matches I.\n(C) Filling of 4f orbitals begins with cerium, $Z = 58$, and ends at Lu (71), so C matches IV.\n(D) Filling of 5f orbitals begins after actinium (89). The actinoids run from Th ($Z = 90$) to Lr, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Among $O^{2-}$, $F^-$, $Na^+$ and $Mg^{2+}$, the smallest species is $Mg^{2+}$.\n(ii) The second ionization enthalpy of sodium is higher than that of magnesium.\n(iii) The Pauling electronegativity of an element has a fixed value regardless of the atom it is bonded to.\n(iv) In $[AlCl(H_2O)_5]^{2+}$, the covalency of Al is numerically equal to its oxidation state.\n(v) Palladium is an exception to the general d-block configuration and has the outer configuration $4d^{10}5s^0$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: all four ions have 10 electrons, and Mg²⁺ has the highest nuclear charge (+12), so it is the smallest.\n(ii) Correct: Na⁺ has a noble-gas core, so removing a second electron needs much more energy. Mg⁺ still has a 3s electron to lose.\n(iii) Incorrect: electronegativity varies with the element the atom is bonded to.\n(iv) Incorrect: the oxidation state of Al is +3, but its covalency is 6.\n(v) Correct: NCERT notes Pd as 4d¹⁰5s⁰.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The second electron gain enthalpy of oxygen, for $O^{-}(g) + e^{-} \\rightarrow O^{2-}(g)$, is positive.\nStatement II: The second electron gain enthalpy of oxygen is more negative than the first, because $O^{2-}$ attains a stable noble gas configuration.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The second electron is added to an already negatively charged ion, $O^{-}$. It must overcome strong electrostatic repulsion, so energy has to be supplied and the value is positive.\nStatement II is incorrect. Reaching a neon-like configuration does not outweigh this repulsion. The second electron gain enthalpy is positive, not more negative than the first value (–141 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Predicted atomic weight of eka-aluminium\n(B) Experimentally found density of gallium\n(C) Experimentally found melting point of germanium\n(D) Predicted atomic weight of eka-silicon\nList-II\n(I) 72\n(II) 1231 K\n(III) 5.94 g/cm$^3$\n(IV) 68","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Eka-aluminium: predicted atomic weight 68, so IV. The found value for Ga is 70.\n(B) Gallium: found density 5.94 g/cm$^3$, so III.\n(C) Germanium: found melting point 1231 K, so II. Mendeleev had predicted 'high'.\n(D) Eka-silicon: predicted atomic weight 72, so I. The found value for Ge is 72.6.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ionic radius of $Na^+$ is much smaller than the atomic radius of Na.\nReason (R): On forming a cation, the nuclear charge of the atom increases.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Na has a radius of 186 pm, while $Na^+$ is only about 95 pm.\nR is false. Removing an electron does not change the number of protons, so the nuclear charge stays the same.\nThe cation is smaller because it has fewer electrons held by that same nuclear charge. Here the whole outer 3s shell is lost.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Dobereiner's Law of Triads was dismissed as a coincidence.\nReason (R): The triad relationship seemed to work for only a few groups of elements.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that Dobereiner's relationship was dismissed as coincidence.\nR is true. The pattern held only for a few sets, such as Li–Na–K, Ca–Sr–Ba and Cl–Br–I.\nR also explains A: because the rule could not be extended to most elements, it was not accepted as a general law.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match the atomic number in List-I with its temporary IUPAC symbol in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) 105\n(B) 109\n(C) 112\n(D) 116\nList-II:\n(I) Uub\n(II) Uuh\n(III) Unp\n(IV) Une","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The roots are: 0 = nil (n), 1 = un (u), 2 = bi (b), 5 = pent (p), 6 = hex (h), 9 = enn (e).\n(A) 105 gives un-nil-pent, so Unp (III).\n(B) 109 gives un-nil-enn, so Une (IV).\n(C) 112 gives un-un-bi, so Uub (I).\n(D) 116 gives un-un-hex, so Uuh (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following oxides behaves as an acid towards bases and as a base towards acids?","image":"","options":["$Na_2O$","$Cl_2O_7$","$Al_2O_3$","$SO_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Oxides of elements at the extreme left of a period are basic. For example, $Na_2O$ reacts with water to give NaOH.\nOxides at the extreme right are acidic. $Cl_2O_7$ gives $HClO_4$, and $SO_3$ is also acidic.\nOxides of elements in the centre of a period, such as $Al_2O_3$, are amphoteric. They react with both acids and bases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (element) with List-II (electron gain enthalpy in kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nList-I: (A) F (B) Cl (C) O (D) Ne\nList-II: (i) –141 (ii) +116 (iii) –349 (iv) –328","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ne is a noble gas, so its value is positive: D-(ii), +116.\nCl has the most negative value of all: B-(iii), –349.\nF is slightly less negative than Cl because of repulsion in its small 2p subshell: A-(iv), –328.\nO is much less negative than F, since it needs two electrons to reach a noble gas configuration and also has compact-shell repulsion: C-(i), –141.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A valence electron experiences an effective nuclear charge smaller than the actual nuclear charge because core electrons shield it.\n(ii) Across the second period, shielding by core electrons increases sharply, so ionization enthalpy decreases from Li to F.\n(iii) Down a group, the increase in shielding outweighs the increase in nuclear charge, so ionization enthalpy decreases.\n(iv) Shielding is most effective when the inner shells are completely filled, as in alkali metals.\n(v) A 2p electron penetrates closer to the nucleus than a 2s electron.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: shielding by core electrons lowers the effective nuclear charge felt by the valence electron.\n(ii) Incorrect: across a period, electrons enter the same shell and core shielding barely changes. Nuclear charge wins, so ionization enthalpy generally increases.\n(iii) Correct.\n(iv) Correct: alkali metals have one ns electron outside a noble-gas core.\n(v) Incorrect: a 2s electron penetrates more than a 2p electron.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the long form of the periodic table, the period number of an element equals the highest principal quantum number (n) of its electrons.\nStatement II: The fourth period contains 18 elements because the 4s, 3d and 4p orbitals, nine orbitals in total, are filled in it.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: each period begins the filling of a new principal shell, so the period number equals the highest n.\nStatement II is correct: in period 4, the 4s (1), 3d (5) and 4p (3) orbitals are filled. These 9 orbitals hold 18 electrons, so the period has 18 elements (K to Kr).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In the compound $OF_2$, what is the oxidation state of oxygen?","image":"","options":["$-2$","$+2$","$-1$","$+1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fluorine is the most electronegative element, so it is given oxidation state $-1$ in every compound.\nThere are two F atoms, so their total is $-2$.\nThe molecule is neutral, so oxygen must be $+2$.\nOxygen shows $-2$ only when it is the more electronegative partner, as in $Na_2O$. Here it is bonded to F, so $-2$ is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"An element has atomic number $Z = 37$. Using its ground-state electronic configuration, identify its position in the long form of the Periodic Table.","image":"","options":["Period 4, Group 1","Period 5, Group 1","Period 5, Group 2","Period 6, Group 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Write the configuration: $Z = 37$ is $[Kr]5s^1$ (krypton has 36 electrons).\nStep 2: The period number equals n of the valence shell. Here n = 5, so the element is in period 5.\nStep 3: A single $ns^1$ valence electron is the alkali-metal configuration, so it is in Group 1. The element is rubidium.\nStep 4: Period 4, Group 1 is potassium ($[Ar]4s^1$). Group 2 would need $5s^2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The number of elements in a period is twice the number of atomic orbitals available in the energy level being filled.\n(ii) The element with Z = 114 lies in the 7th period and group 14.\n(iii) Zn, Cd and Hg show most of the typical properties of transition elements.\n(iv) Palladium has the outer electronic configuration 4d$^{10}$5s$^0$.\n(v) Elements after uranium are called transuranium elements.\n(vi) The period number corresponds to the azimuthal quantum number of the valence shell.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Each orbital holds 2 electrons; for example, the 9 orbitals filled in period 5 give 18 elements.\n(ii) Correct. Element 114 has the configuration [Rn]5f$^{14}$6d$^{10}$7s$^2$7p$^2$, which places it in period 7, group 14.\n(iii) Incorrect. Zn, Cd and Hg have (n-1)d$^{10}$ns$^2$ configurations and lack most transition properties.\n(iv) Correct. This is the stated exception for Pd.\n(v) Correct.\n(vi) Incorrect. The period number equals the principal quantum number n.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the correct increasing order of the first ionization enthalpy of the group 13 elements?","image":"","options":["$Tl < In < Ga < Al < B$","$In < Al < Ga < Tl < B$","$Al < Ga < In < Tl < B$","$In < Ga < Al < Tl < B$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The values are B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558 and Tl 589 kJ mol$^{-1}$, so the order is In < Al < Ga < Tl < B.\nB is the smallest atom, so it has by far the highest value.\nGa is slightly higher than Al because its 3d electrons shield poorly.\nIn follows the normal downward trend and has the lowest value.\nTl rises again because its 4f and 5d electrons shield very poorly, giving a higher effective nuclear charge.\nOption (1) wrongly assumes a smooth decrease down the group.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The first four successive ionization enthalpies (in kJ mol$^{-1}$) of a main group element X are 738, 1451, 7733 and 10540. What is the most likely formula of its stable chloride?","image":"","options":["$XCl$","$XCl_2$","$XCl_3$","$XCl_4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Look for the large jump in successive ionization enthalpies.\nThe jump from 1451 to 7733 kJ mol$^{-1}$ is about fivefold. It occurs on removing the third electron.\nThis means the third electron comes from a noble-gas core, so X has only 2 valence electrons and belongs to group 2.\nX therefore forms $X^{2+}$, and its chloride is $XCl_2$.\nThe data match magnesium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $Mg^{2+}$ is smaller than $Na^+$.\n(ii) The covalent radius of chlorine is taken as half the Cl–Cl bond distance in $Cl_2$.\n(iii) In group 17, the increase in atomic radius from F to Cl is larger than the increase from Cl to Br.\n(iv) Atomic radius decreases from Na to Cl mainly because shielding by inner-core electrons increases sharply across the period.\n(v) A cation is smaller than its parent atom because its nuclear charge increases on losing electrons.\n(vi) The radius of neon is best compared with the covalent radius of fluorine.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the two ions are isoelectronic, and Mg has the higher nuclear charge.\n(ii) Correct: 198/2 = 99 pm.\n(iii) Correct: F→Cl is 64→99 (+35 pm), while Cl→Br is 99→114 (+15 pm).\n(iv) Incorrect: shielding hardly changes across a period. The rising nuclear charge is what shrinks the atom.\n(v) Incorrect: the nuclear charge stays the same; the cation simply has fewer electrons.\n(vi) Incorrect: Ne's radius is a van der Waals radius and should be compared with van der Waals radii.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about f-block elements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["All actinoid elements are radioactive.","The chemistry of early actinoids is more complicated than that of the corresponding lanthanoids because of the larger number of possible oxidation states.","Within each of the lanthanoid and actinoid series, the properties of the elements are quite similar.","The elements following actinium in the periodic table are called transuranium elements."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that actinoid elements are radioactive.\n(2) Correct: early actinoids show many oxidation states, which makes their chemistry more complicated than that of the lanthanoids.\n(3) Correct: properties within each series are quite similar.\n(4) Incorrect: transuranium elements are those that come after uranium (Z = 92), not after actinium (Z = 89). Th, Pa and U follow Ac but are not transuranium elements.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Mendeleev's work are correct?\n(i) He relied on similarities in the empirical formulas and properties of compounds formed by elements.\n(ii) His Periodic Law was published before Lothar Meyer's table was published.\n(iii) His table arranged elements by atomic number as revealed by X-ray spectra.\n(iv) He predicted the oxide of eka-silicon to have the formula EO$_2$.\n(v) His classification was based only on plots of atomic volume against atomic weight.\n(vi) He placed iodine after tellurium although iodine has the lower atomic weight.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: he used empirical formulas and properties of compounds.\n(ii) Correct: Lothar Meyer's 1868 table was published only after Mendeleev's work.\n(iii) Incorrect: atomic number came from Moseley in 1913. Mendeleev used atomic weights.\n(iv) Correct: he predicted EO$_2$, and germanium forms GeO$_2$.\n(v) Incorrect: plotting atomic volume against atomic weight was Lothar Meyer's approach. Mendeleev used a broad range of properties.\n(vi) Correct: he placed I in Group VII for its similar properties.\nCorrect statements: (i), (ii), (iv) and (vi), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":172,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following elements in increasing order of the magnitude of their negative electron gain enthalpy (least negative first):\nO, S, Se, F, Cl","image":"","options":["O < Se < S < F < Cl","Se < S < O < Cl < F","O < S < Se < Cl < F","S < O < Se < F < Cl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The NCERT values (kJ mol$^{-1}$) are O –141, Se –195, S –200, F –328 and Cl –349.\nHalogens are far more negative than chalcogens, because gaining one electron completes their octet.\nWithin each group, the second-period member (O, F) is less negative than the third-period member (S, Cl), due to repulsion in the compact n = 2 shell.\nBelow S, the values become less negative down the group as size increases, so Se is slightly less negative than S.\nThe order is therefore O < Se < S < F < Cl.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":173,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Mendeleev left a gap below silicon in his table and named the missing element eka-silicon. Which element was later found to occupy this position?","image":"","options":["Gallium","Germanium","Scandium","Tin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mendeleev left two gaps: one under aluminium (eka-aluminium) and one under silicon (eka-silicon).\nEka-silicon was later discovered as germanium. Its found oxide GeO$_2$ and chloride GeCl$_4$ match his predictions.\nGallium is the tempting wrong option, but gallium filled the eka-aluminium gap.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":174,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about John Newlands and his Law of Octaves are correct?\n(i) He was an English chemist.\n(ii) He arranged elements in increasing order of their atomic weights.\n(iii) He compared the repetition of properties to the eighth note resembling the first in a musical octave.\n(iv) His idea was widely accepted as soon as it was proposed.\n(v) He was later awarded the Davy Medal by the Royal Society, London.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Newlands was an English chemist.\n(ii) Correct: he arranged elements by increasing atomic weight.\n(iii) Correct: he made the musical-octave analogy.\n(iv) Incorrect: his idea was not widely accepted at that time.\n(v) Correct: he received the Davy Medal in 1887 from the Royal Society, London.\nThat makes 4 correct statements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":175,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mendeleev's Periodic Law had to be modified after Moseley showed that atomic number is a more fundamental property of an element than its atomic mass.\nStatement II: Moseley obtained a straight line when he plotted the square root of the X-ray frequency against atomic mass.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: Moseley's results established atomic number as more fundamental, and this led to the Modern Periodic Law.\nStatement II is incorrect: the straight line came from $\\sqrt{\\nu}$ plotted against atomic number Z. The plot against atomic mass was not linear.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":176,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"The bond length in the $Cl_2$ molecule is 198 pm and in the $Br_2$ molecule is 228 pm. Using the idea of covalent radius, what is the expected bond length in a BrCl molecule?","image":"","options":["213 pm","426 pm","198 pm","228 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: d(Cl–Cl) = 198 pm and d(Br–Br) = 228 pm.\nFormula: The covalent radius is half the homonuclear bond length. The bond length of A–B is approximately r(A) + r(B).\nSubstituting: r(Cl) = 198/2 = 99 pm and r(Br) = 228/2 = 114 pm. So d(Br–Cl) = 99 + 114 = 213 pm.\nCheck of a wrong option: 426 pm is the sum of the two full bond lengths; it forgets to halve them.\nSo the expected Br–Cl bond length = 213 pm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":177,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In the long form of the Periodic Table, the period number of an element corresponds to the:","image":"","options":["highest principal quantum number (n) of the element","azimuthal quantum number of the last electron","number of valence electrons","number of filled subshells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"There are seven periods in the long form.\nEach new period begins with the filling of the next higher principal energy level.\nSo the period number equals the highest principal quantum number n of the element; for example, K (4s$^1$) is in period 4.\nThe azimuthal quantum number of the last electron decides the block (s, p, d or f), not the period.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-PERIODICITY-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Classification of Elements and Periodicity","qno":178,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","type":"mcq","question":"In the graph of first ionization enthalpy plotted against atomic number for elements with Z = 1 to 60, the sharp peaks and the deepest troughs correspond respectively to:","image":"","options":["Halogens and alkaline earth metals","Noble gases and alkali metals","Alkali metals and noble gases","Transition metals and lanthanoids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: The peaks (maxima) occur at the noble gases. Their closed, very stable valence shells make an electron hard to remove.\nStep 2: The troughs (minima) occur at the alkali metals. They have a single ns electron outside a noble-gas core, so it is lost easily, which matches their high reactivity.\nStep 3: Halogens have high values, but they sit just before the peaks, not at them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 3 · Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","difficulty":"easy"}]});
